export const EVIDENCE = {
  "alpha-theta": {"title": "Alpha-theta training", "grade": "weak", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet run on a phone). Eyes-closed training at the back of the head: a stream sound for alpha (the relaxed 8-12 Hz wave) crossfades to ocean waves for theta (the slower 4-8 Hz drowsy, dream-like wave), with a soft high chime and a low gong. Ten sessions, the first three only 15 minutes, then decide.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: weak</h3><p>The headband sits where the classic protocol was first done (O1), but alpha-theta&#x27;s reputation rests on tiny studies wrapped in therapy, and it has the most reported side effects of the common feedback protocols.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>The famous Peniston studies used O1 with about ten patients per group; a 520-person prison study found little difference at 2 years.</li><li>Documented side effects: emotional swings, spacey feelings, evoked traumatic memories, and clients who drove onto a curb, through a red light and into a light pole after a first session; once a seizure after slow-wave reward. Even healthy volunteers report some side effects.</li><li>The &quot;crossover&quot; moment (theta overtakes alpha) looks a lot like falling asleep: stage-2 sleep (ordinary light sleep) filled 10-14% of training time, and at most 13% of people could tell they had slept.</li><li>For PTSD, depression and addiction, only small, old or uncontrolled studies are positive; the best PTSD EEG trial was not better than fake feedback.</li><li>Studies in healthy people used about 10 sessions.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>Treating anything. It is offered as practice, not treatment, and not at all to people with PTSD, dissociation, psychosis, epilepsy, active addiction or severe depression.</li><li>A large (250-person) fake-controlled PTSD trial reports only in 2027.</li><li>No published threshold values exist from the original protocols; every number below is a planning value.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Its own screen, opened from the meditation screen. Before first use: nine screening questions (any &quot;yes&quot; and the mode is not offered; nothing you tick is saved), plain consent (it can bring up strong memories, leave you drowsy or spacey, and cause tiredness, headache, anxiety or poor sleep; at least 1 in 5 get no benefit; practice, not treatment), and your stop plan: someone to call and what grounds you.</li><li>Before each session a check-in: sleepiness, distress 0-10, and a side-effect checklist for the time since the last session. In the first three check-ins a 1-minute reaction test sets your usual speed.</li><li>Calibration as in meditation: 30 s eyes open, 60 s eyes closed in silence (the research suggests 1 + 2 min; the shorter one is a planning choice). Alpha is your peak ± 2 Hz; theta is 6 to 2 Hz below your peak.</li><li>Sessions: 15 min in the first three, then 20 or 25 min; after ten the app asks you to decide before going on. Each band&#x27;s line starts at your silent minute&#x27;s middle value and moves every 3 minutes, aiming at alpha above it 50-65 % of the time and theta 30-40 %. A chime after 1 s of alpha above its line, a gong after 1 s of theta above its line, at most one per band every 2.5 s, theta winning ties; a jaw clench, movement or blink earns nothing and ducks the sound a little.</li><li>Sleep guard: when the drift-off detector says &quot;probably asleep&quot;, the sound fades and a soft rising tone wakes you, again every minute while it lasts. Those minutes are shown and left out of every measure. Crossover (theta above alpha) is shown for awake time only, as a fact, never as a goal.</li><li>Stop is always on the screen and never asks: the sound fades over about 10 s. Then a minute of eyes-open grounding, then the questions: distress, strong memories (none, pleasant, distressing), feeling unreal or detached, anything like a seizure, the checklist again, how in control you felt, and notes.</li><li>Before you leave: a sleepiness rating and a 1-minute reaction test against your usual. If slower by over 10 % or sleepy: &quot;do not drive or cycle yet&quot;, and a retest after 10 minutes.</li><li>The mode pauses and suggests talking to a professional after distress of 7/10 or more, two sessions in a row with distressing memories, anything like dissociation, a side effect lasting into the next day, or any seizure-like event (then a doctor first). It resumes only when you confirm you have talked to someone.</li><li>With the replay check on, alpha-theta has its own check (the same rules as meditation&#x27;s).</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://isnr-jnt.org/article/view/16682/10648\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hammond &amp; Kirk 2008: side effects</a></li><li><a href=\"https://isnr-jnt.org/article/view/16583/10550\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISNR standards 2011</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2524976/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Peniston &amp; Kulkosky 1989</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2259255/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sokhadze 2008 review</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25835580/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Schutze &amp; Junghanns 2015: sleep during alpha-theta</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26008757/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Rogel 2015: side effects in healthy volunteers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10090479/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nicholson 2023: PTSD trial</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15820636/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Raymond 2005: dose in healthy people</a></li><li><a href=\"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06770998\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT06770998: PTSD trial, results due 2027</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ellipse-marlin-482e.squarespace.com/eeger-answers/alpha-theta-basics\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EEGer alpha-theta basics (sound design)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own feedback core (MIT), shared with the brain sound and meditation feedback.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/mne-rt-org/mne-rt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MNE-RT</a> (BSD-3): open feedback protocols, used as a reference.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "brain-sound": {"title": "Brain sound (alpha-driven sound and bells)", "grade": "weak", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Rain and a low drone that follow the alpha rhythm at the back of your head (the 8-12 Hz wave that grows when you close your eyes and settle). When alpha rises above your own line, the rain softens and the drone warms; stay there a few seconds and a bell rings. A tight jaw, a blink or drifting toward sleep pauses the bells.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: weak</h3><p>The headband measures alpha well, but in careful trials sound that follows your brain has rarely beaten sound that follows a recording (fake feedback), so the benefit of the feedback itself is weak.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Real neurofeedback (sound or pictures that follow your brain) usually ties with fake feedback for ADHD, insomnia and anxiety, even when people really learn to move the trained signal. Both groups get better, so the quiet practice itself seems to be what helps.</li><li>Alpha rises over a session with fake feedback and with no feedback at all, so a rising line is not learning.</li><li>Delay matters: in the one direct test, 0.24 s from brain to sound trained best and 0.74 s left no lasting effect. It has not been repeated by another group.</li><li>A target that tightens slowly between blocks did better than one reset every 30 seconds, which did no better than placebo.</li><li>Muscle is the classic false reward: in one study half the people trained to raise a fast brain rhythm raised their muscle tension instead.</li><li>Pooled over 16 trials in healthy people, alpha feedback improved working memory by a medium amount (effect size 0.56), but trials with a fake-feedback control often found no difference.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>No study shows that the brain-linked sound, rather than sitting quietly with your eyes closed, is what helps. It is not a treatment for anxiety, sleep problems or anything else.</li><li>The bell count is not a score of how calm you are.</li><li>Bluetooth headphones add about 0.15-0.5 s, which loses the speed that training mode is built for.</li><li>About 0.2-0.25 s from brain to ear with wired headphones or a speaker is an estimate until it is measured on your phone and headphones.</li><li>No study compares rain, tones and music as feedback sounds; the &quot;noise clears as alpha rises&quot; design rests on a study of 10 people.</li><li>None of the thresholds below has been tested on a real head with this headband yet: they are planning values.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Silent calibration: 30 s eyes open, then 60 s eyes closed. It finds your alpha peak; alpha is measured at your peak ± 2 Hz.</li><li>Your line is the level your eyes-closed alpha exceeds about 65% of the time. It is held still for 3-minute blocks; after a block above it over 80% of the time it rises a little, under 50% it falls a little.</li><li>A bell needs at least 3 s above the line within the last 4 s and no gate in those 4 s; then at least 5 s (training) or 15 s (relax) until the next, and never more than 6 a minute.</li><li>Gates: jaw or neck muscle over about twice its resting level; a blink; drifting toward sleep (slow theta waves rising against alpha, the alpha peak slowing by 1 Hz, or alpha falling under half its starting level). Any gate means no bells and a neutral sound, and the moment does not count.</li><li>Training mode answers in about 0.3 s (use wired headphones or the speaker); relax mode glides over about 1 s, rings more rarely and is fine with Bluetooth.</li><li>Every session is logged and can be replayed exactly; this is the base of the replay check.</li><li>The page says it plainly: a relaxing tool, not a treatment.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13745793\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wang 2026: ADHD, double-blind</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39661381/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Westwood 2025 meta-analysis</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5382955/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Schabus 2017: insomnia, real vs placebo training</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/psyp.70285\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Maaz 2026: alpha rises with any feedback</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10046\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Maaz 2025 preprint: double-blind follow-up</a></li><li><a href=\"https://publications.hse.ru/mirror/pubs/share/direct/424296808.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Belinskaia 2020: feedback delay test</a></li><li><a href=\"https://experts.mcmaster.ca/scholarly-works/1614032\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dhindsa 2018: slow tightening</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3051071\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lansbergen 2011: fast auto-threshold failed</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fnhum.2017.00119/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Paluch 2017: muscle mistaken for brain</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fnhum.2020.562360/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Yeh 2021 meta-analysis</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29016171/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Thibault &amp; Raz 2017: why both groups improve</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8553721\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Takabatake 2021: noise over music that clears</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.soundguys.com/android-bluetooth-latency-22732/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SoundGuys: Bluetooth delay on Android</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>No outside library in the page: the browser&#x27;s built-in Web Audio makes the sound; the feedback core is this project&#x27;s own code (MIT).</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/alnitak/flutter_soloud\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_soloud</a> (MIT): the sound engine for the phone app.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/google/oboe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Oboe</a> (Apache-2.0): Android&#x27;s low-delay audio library.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/mne-rt-org/mne-rt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MNE-RT</a> (BSD-3): open neurofeedback protocols, used as a reference.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "break-hints": {"title": "Break hints", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>A small laptop program that suggests a short break after about an hour of continuous keyboard and mouse activity. It reads ActivityWatch&#x27;s activity log; it does not use the headband.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Short breaks reliably lift energy and lower tiredness a little, but no brain-based break detector has independent proof, so the hints come from activity time only.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Short breaks raise vigour and cut tiredness (small effects: d about 0.35, an effect size where 0.2 is small and 0.5 medium) but do not reliably improve performance on hard work.</li><li>A fixed 25-minutes-on, 5-off timer did no better than breaks people chose themselves in a 2025 trial, and tiredness rose faster under it.</li><li>No company has independent proof for a brain-based break prompt; the best-known one has only internal data, and reviewers called its prompts &quot;nagging&quot;.</li><li>Computer activity logs explain only about 9% of how developers rated their day; mood explained 12% of productivity ratings and the time of day under 1%.</li><li>Counting focus sessions (runs of related work of about 35-60 minutes) tracked how often engineers felt focused; total hours did not.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>The hints do not know how tired you are; time at the keyboard is only a stand-in.</li><li>These four electrodes cannot give a &quot;focus %&quot; while coding: mental effort shows at the front and top of the head, and even research-grade electrodes around the ear saw only weak links.</li><li>The popular &quot;52/17&quot; rule is a blog statistic that changed when it was rerun.</li><li>The 60-minute start, the 4-a-day cap and the back-off are sensible choices, not values from research.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Every minute it reads ActivityWatch&#x27;s away-from-keyboard data. Gaps under 3 min count as work (reading, thinking); 3-5 min gaps pause the count; 5 min or more away resets it (that was a break).</li><li>After 60 min of work: one quiet notification with &quot;Snooze 20 min&quot; and &quot;OK&quot;.</li><li>At most 4 a day (a day runs 04:00 to 04:00), at least 45 min apart; each hint that did not lead to a break adds 15 min for the rest of the day; nothing between 23:00 and 08:00.</li><li>Every hint and what followed is saved on your computer for later statistics. Nothing leaves the laptop.</li><li>Later stages, in order: your own quick ratings, then typing rhythm (timing only, never which key), then a drowsiness trend from the headband.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://doaj.org/article/c6f37b6ba159469296b2cb968b65505f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Micro-break meta-analysis (Albulescu 2022)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12292963/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pomodoro vs self-chosen breaks (Smits 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.androidcentral.com/accessories/headphones/neurable-mw75-neuro-brain-computer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Android Central on Neurable&#x27;s prompts</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07428\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Developer productivity telemetry (Beller 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://aserebre.win.tue.nl/TSE2021.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Time of day vs productivity ratings (Girardi 2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.04711\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Focus sessions (Brown 2023)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8037053\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code effort sits at the front of the head (Medeiros 2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://publikationen.bibliothek.kit.edu/1000183587\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Around-ear EEG and flow (Knierim, CHI 2025)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/ActivityWatch/activitywatch/releases\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ActivityWatch</a> (MPL-2.0): logs keyboard and mouse activity on the laptop.</li><li>Python&#x27;s standard library and the desktop&#x27;s <code>notify-send</code>; no model.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "dream-catcher": {"title": "Dream catcher", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Built in the phone app (2026-11-18), not yet tried on a head. As you drift off, the app notices the half-dream moment (the alpha wave at the back of the head fading), quietly asks &quot;what&#x27;s going through your mind?&quot;, records your spoken answer, and lets you drift again: 3-4 rounds. For creative ideas.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Two small but careful studies back the half-dream moment for ideas, and its main sign (alpha fading at the back electrodes, O1/O2) is what this headband sees best; spotting the moment live from the EEG is weak, and a simple timer works about as well.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>People who reached the lightest sleep stage (N1, the drowsy first stage) during a 20-minute rest found a hidden puzzle shortcut 83% of the time (20 of 24), against 31% of those who stayed awake and 14% of those who went into deeper sleep. The sweet spot was measured at O1, an electrode this headband has.</li><li>MIT&#x27;s Dormio (49 adults): a theme cue played at sleep onset gave the most creative stories. It sensed sleep onset from hand muscle, pulse and skin, not from EEG.</li><li>A timer-only version reached the half-dream state in 91% of 80 people, against 92% with sensors.</li><li>Headbands with electrodes at these sites time sleep onset within a few minutes: one found onset within 2.5 minutes on 96% of 49 nights.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>The studies cannot prove that the light sleep caused the insight.</li><li>Live detection of the lightest sleep stage is weak in every system found (for example 34% in one live scorer).</li><li>The app&#x27;s alpha rule matched the sleep model within half a minute on the only two recorded sleep onsets, both abrupt; the sleep model reads the same alpha, and 3 more nights are needed before the rule is trusted.</li><li>It is a creativity exercise, not a sleep aid or therapy.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Its own screen, opened from the main screen between nights; it holds the headband while it runs. The screen stays on but black and as dim as the phone allows; keep the app open.</li><li>Once: you record two short prompts in your own voice. The theme (&quot;Think of a tree... and drift off&quot;) plays at lights off and after each answer, as you drift off (Dormio&#x27;s incubation); the question plays at each round. Not recorded: a low tone and two rising notes instead.</li><li>&quot;Lights off&quot; starts the shared drift-off detector (docs/detectors.md): the first 60 eyes-closed seconds are your starting level. The first question comes when alpha has stayed under half that level for a minute (30-60 s after it flattened), with no movement and no blinks.</li><li>A timer is always the fallback: a random moment inside your usual time to fall asleep (10-20 minutes until the app has 3 of your own first rounds).</li><li>One round: the question; your answer recorded until 3 s of silence (or 10 s without a voice, or 30 s); a one-tap &quot;were you drifting?&quot; (two big halves of the screen); then the theme again: &quot;you can drift off again&quot;. The next round waits for alpha to come back and fade again, at least 2 minutes later, or 4-7 minutes at most.</li><li>3 or 4 rounds and a 45-minute cap. At the end, your choice: a gentle rising wake tone, or no tone and the night recording starts by itself.</li><li>The session&#x27;s EEG is saved as part of that night, with every question as a marker, so the morning sleep report covers the drift-off and the detector can be checked against the sleep model.</li><li>Afterwards the screen lists the rounds: play each answer and type a note.</li><li>Your answers as words: once you download the speech model (671 MB, once, from the dream catcher&#x27;s <strong>Answers as text</strong>), the phone writes each answer out after the session, never during it, Russian or English without a setting. It runs on the phone; the answers never leave it. The words sit under each answer&#x27;s play button; fix any word in its box (your version is kept, the phone&#x27;s too). On spoken test clips the model got about 1 word in 20 wrong in Russian (1 in 10 when quiet, slow and muffled) and fewer in English; a real sleepy mumble will do worse, so the recording stays the original.</li><li>The detector ships only when its sleep onset falls within 5 minutes of the sleep model&#x27;s on 4 of 5 recorded nights (2 so far on the laptop, both within half a minute); until then the timers make every round happen anyway.</li><li>The same detector runs during every night the phone records: tap <strong>Lights off</strong> on the night screen as you close your eyes (a night without the tap counts from its start). The morning sleep report shows &quot;Falling asleep&quot;: the minutes from lights off to sleep by the detector and by the sleep model, whether they agree within 5 minutes, and how many of your nights so far agree. A dream catcher session&#x27;s lights off counts too when the night follows it. Both are guesses, so the check shows that they agree, not that either is right.</li><li>Optional later: a home &quot;insight test&quot; that rotates prompted rest, unprompted rest and awake rest on different days.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8654287\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lacaux 2021: 83% vs 14% found the puzzle shortcut</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.sciencenews.org/article/sleep-creativity-neuroscience-brain\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Science News critique</a></li><li><a href=\"https://news.mit.edu/2023/sleep-sweet-spot-dreams-creativity-0515\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MIT Dormio 2023</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/frsle.2024.1258345/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dormio Light: a timer works as well</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.02592\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Earable: sleep onset on the phone</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.06.02.657451v1.full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ezscore-f: live light-sleep detection</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12530990/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hori sleep-onset scale review (Ogilvie 2001)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own drift-off detector (MIT), grown from the brain sound&#x27;s sleep guard; no machine-learning model.</li><li>The microphone: <code>record</code> 7.2.0 (BSD-3-Clause); answers are kept as sound files on the phone.</li><li>Speech to text, on the phone: sherpa-onnx 2.0.1 (Apache-2.0, k2-fsa) running NVIDIA&#x27;s Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 (CC-BY-4.0, 25 European languages including Russian and English; downloaded when you ask, 671 MB), with Silero&#x27;s voice detector (MIT) so breaths and room noise are not turned into words. Checked on the laptop with the same engine and model (<code>scripts/dream_text_check.py</code>): word error rate 5 % (Russian) and 1 % (English) on clear test clips, 9 % and 2 % on quiet, slow, muffled ones, no words made up from silence. Whisper small, the usual choice, was twice as wrong in Russian and invented words in every silent clip without the voice detector.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "eyes-test": {"title": "Eyes open or closed", "grade": "strong", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>A live test of the back electrodes: a high beep while your eyes are open, a low beep while they are closed, worked out from the alpha rhythm (the 8-12 Hz wave at the back of the head that grows when you close your eyes). Calibrate once: 10 s eyes open, then 10 s eyes closed.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: strong</h3><p>Alpha at the back of the head jumps when the eyes close, one of the most reliable effects in EEG, and this headband&#x27;s back electrodes match lab electrodes.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>A dry headband with exactly this layout matched lab gel electrodes for back-of-head alpha (correlation 0.90 in 19 adults). The side electrodes (T3, T4) showed no eyes-closed alpha rise on either system, so only the back pair is used.</li><li>About one person in ten shows little or no alpha rise on closing the eyes; this test tells you whether you are one of them.</li><li>Drowsiness flips the rule: with eyes open, rising alpha can mean you are getting sleepy; with eyes closed, falling alpha means you are drifting off.</li><li>The reference electrode sits on the forehead, so blinks show on all four channels and can look like a jump.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>It reads alpha, not your eyelids: drowsy with eyes open can look &quot;closed&quot;, and dozing with eyes closed can look &quot;open&quot;.</li><li>It is a signal check and a demonstration, not a measure of relaxation or focus.</li><li>With poor contact at the back electrodes, open and closed look alike.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Four times a second: alpha power (8-12 Hz) against its neighbours (4-6 and 15-20 Hz) over the last second, at O1 and O2, on a log scale. Using neighbours makes it robust to how loud the whole signal is.</li><li>Calibration takes the middle value of the eyes-open and eyes-closed seconds and puts the line half-way. If the two differ by less than about 1.4 times, the page says they look alike and to check contact.</li><li>A small margin on each side of the line stops flicker. A flat or very large signal (over 400 µV: movement or a blink) shows &quot;…&quot; instead of a guess.</li><li>The same eyes-closed alpha feeds the morning check-in, the brain sound and the alpha bar on the headband check page.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11679099/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mikhaylov 2024: dry headband vs gel, same layout</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537023/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Alpha basics (StatPearls)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/905755554\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sleepiness ratings vs EEG (Kaida 2006)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://sdk.brainbit.com/device-recommendation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BrainBit device notes (forehead reference)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>None: plain JavaScript in the page; the beeps use the browser&#x27;s built-in Web Audio.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "hands-free": {"title": "Hands-free controls", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Off unless you turn it on. A deliberate double jaw clench (two short clenches 0.3-1.5 s apart, then nothing for 2 seconds) or a look left, then right, works as a switch during a session: pause or resume the sound, mark a moment, or answer &quot;were you drifting?&quot;. A short calibration sets the levels for your head.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Jaw clenches at T3/T4 are among the clearest things this headband sees, and jaw and eye switches near the ears work in small lab studies; but no study has tested them as switches on this headband, almost none counted false triggers over hours of real use, and telling left from right is the weaker part (62% on this headband in one study).</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Muscle switches (EMG: the muscle signal) work, but fire by accident more often than buttons. In 12 people with disabilities trying a dry-sensor muscle switch, the 8 who also used a button never had fewer accidental presses with the muscle switch; the one person wearing it on the jaw made 2.9 presses for every intended one (button: 1). A European gaze-control report adds that coughing or laughing raises the muscle signal and can fire one or more clicks.</li><li>A single clench is a poor switch: temple-muscle recordings in 34 people found 33.6 clench episodes an hour by day, and 23% of people who thought they did not clench did.</li><li>Asking for a pattern of actions, like a wake word, cuts false triggers to near zero at the cost of misses. A triple tooth tap, sensed by motion and sound in an earpiece, gave no false triggers in 71.5 minutes of 11 people talking, writing, walking and eating, but missed 23 of 133 real taps (17%). A forearm muscle &quot;wake gesture&quot; ignored over 99.9% of everyday movements and missed 19-25% of real ones; people preferred misses to false triggers. A commercial earbud (Wisear) chose a double jaw clench for pause and resume; about 80% of people at a trade-show demo made it work, with no false-trigger numbers published.</li><li>Eye switches (EOG: the eye-movement signal) show the same: in separate lab studies (not a fair head-to-head), a single blink falsely fired 0.18 times a minute (about 11 an hour), a double blink 0.26-0.74 a minute (16-44 an hour, 16 people), and a triple blink 0.01 a minute (under 1 an hour, 11 people). The authors warn that tired users may mix up deliberate and automatic blinks.</li><li>Near the ears: headphones with six dry electrodes around the ears told left glance, right glance, jaw clench and rest apart 94% of the time (97% with a model trained on the person) in 19 healthy people steering a simulated wheelchair. In another ear-electrode study (16 people) a 15° sideways glance swung about 40 µV, while up and down were unusable. On this headband, 22 people&#x27;s eye jumps were found 90% of the time, but left was told from right only 62% of the time (50% is guessing); those were small natural eye movements at a screen, not big deliberate glances.</li><li>The &quot;Midas touch&quot; problem (the king whose touch turned everything to gold): eyes move all the time without meaning anything, so an interface that acts on every look soon fires everywhere. The classic fix is to ask for something deliberate, such as holding the look or a second action; a long hold avoids accidents but feels slow, and a short one is fine only when a wrong pick is undone at once.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>No study has tested jaw or eye switches on this headband, and none measured false triggers over hours of real use (the longest everyday test above was 71.5 minutes across 11 people). The app&#x27;s own check is one recorded night and one morning check-in where nobody made a gesture (below); whether it catches your real gestures is unknown until your calibration.</li><li>Left versus right is the weak part on this headband (62%). Big deliberate glances should be clearer than the small movements in that study, but that is untested here. In the headphone study the dry electrodes needed 10-20 minutes to make good contact, and the failed commands all came with poor contact.</li><li>Drowsiness fakes the eye gesture: while drifting off, the eyes make slow, rolling, mostly sideways swings (20-200 µV, each taking 1-10 seconds). They belong to drowsy waking and the lightest sleep and grow in the minutes before deeper light sleep, so they come while you may still count as awake. On T3 minus T4 they look like left-right glances, just when a dream-induction session asks &quot;were you drifting?&quot;. They are slower than a deliberate glance, which may help tell them apart, but that is untested; a glance answer near sleep is not reliable.</li><li>Sleep fakes the jaw gesture: nearly 60% of healthy sleepers show rhythmic chewing-muscle bursts in sleep (1-6 episodes an hour on average in young adults), and people swallow about 2-6 times an hour asleep in different studies, almost always (98%) during a brief awakening. No numbers were found for jaw twitches in dream sleep in people.</li><li>Coughing, laughing, talking and chewing also light up the jaw muscle band; the quiet time around a double clench is meant to reject them, but that has not been measured.</li><li>Most numbers come from healthy young adults sitting still in a lab, often with gel electrodes; one jaw-clench detector on wet ear electrodes was right only 72% of the times it said &quot;clench&quot; against 26 other face movements (one person).</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Off until you switch it on in Settings (the gear on the main screen). Switching on first runs the calibration, about 90 seconds with the headband: 30 seconds sitting still, then three &quot;clench twice now&quot; and three &quot;look left, then right&quot;, each on a soft tone. It sets the clench line at half your weakest calibrated clench (kept between 10 and 60 times your resting level), the look size at 0.6 of your smallest calibrated look (70-250 µV), and which way is left when all tries agree. A gesture is used only if at least 2 of its 3 tries were caught.</li><li>Double clench: two bursts of 0.2-1 s, 0.3-1.5 s apart, with both temple electrodes over the line at each burst&#x27;s strongest tenth of a second; no other jaw muscle (both sides up) for 2 seconds before and after, and no movement or lost signal. Muscle is the 55-120 Hz band every 0.1 s against the detectors&#x27; own resting level (the quietest fifth of the last 2 minutes).</li><li>Look left, then right: two opposite steps of T3 minus T4 of at least the look size, each within 1.5 s of the one before (a third, back to the middle, may follow), and no other eye movement for 2 seconds around. Once left is known the first step must go left; until then either way counts (&quot;look away and back&quot;). The steps are changes over 0.1 s, so the slow drowsy eye rolls above mostly do not count; untested.</li><li>What they do: Sound and Meditation: a double clench pauses the sound and the next brings it back; a look marks a moment. Dream catcher: while &quot;were you drifting?&quot; waits, a double clench answers Drifting (a nod) and a look answers Awake (a shake of the head); otherwise a moment. Alpha-theta and the nap coach: a moment. The night recording: only the double clench, as a moment marker without a tone (dream sleep is full of left-right eye movements).</li><li>A soft tone confirms each gesture, 2-4 seconds after it (the app waits for the quiet 2 seconds). Gestures are off while the drift-off detector says you are probably asleep (except while the dream catcher&#x27;s question waits for its answer: the question has just woken you), and never in the morning check-in, so they cannot change its measurement.</li><li>Checked where nobody made a gesture, at the first-guess settings (line 10 times rest, looks of 80 µV either way): on one recorded night, awake 4.6 hours: no double clench and 9 looks (about 2 an hour; about 1 an hour with left known); asleep 6.5 hours: 4 double clenches and 4 looks (about 0.6 an hour each); a 5-minute morning check-in: none. With a clench line of 40 times rest or more, none came up asleep either.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/226714\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dry-sensor muscle switch in 12 users (Judge 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.wiki.cogain.info/images/d/d1/COGAIN-D4.9.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">COGAIN report on muscle switches with gaze (2009)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/josnusd/66/1/66_23-0362/_article\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Daytime clenching (Yoshida et al. 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.12548\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TeethTap: triple tooth tap as a wake gesture (Sun et al. 2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2402.10050\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wake gestures against false triggers (Eddy et al. 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2022/01/20/wisear/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wisear double-clench earbuds (TechCrunch 2022)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7714925\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Triple-blink switch, single-blink comparison (Zhu et al. 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957419\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Double-blink switch (Wang et al. 2022)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.yorku.ca/mack/etra2025.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EarEOG: dry headphone electrodes, glances and clench (Liu et al. 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2506.07193v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sideways glances on ear electrodes (King et al. 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://fruct.org/files/publications/volume-36/fruct36/Rom.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eye direction on this headband (Romaniuk &amp; Kashevnik 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://marcjohnson.fr/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/1991-Jacob-The-Use-of-Eye-Movements-in-human-computer-interaction-techniques.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Midas touch problem (Jacob 1991)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.scitepress.org/Papers/2013/45513/45513.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Slow eye movements while falling asleep (Cona et al. 2013)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://read.qxmd.com/read/11332529/rhythmic-masticatory-muscle-activity-during-sleep-in-humans\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Chewing-muscle bursts in normal sleepers (Lavigne et al. 2001)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://read.qxmd.com/read/30703175/sleep-quality-psychologic-profiles-cardiac-activity-and-salivary-biomarkers-in-young-subjects-with-different-degrees-of-rhythmic-masticatory-muscle-activity-a-polysomnography-study\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Chewing-muscle bursts per hour in young adults (Haraki et al. 2019)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7165485\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Swallowing in sleep comes with awakenings (Burke et al. 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/fmj/10/2/10_2023-010/_html/-char/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Swallows per hour asleep, earlier studies (Ito et al. 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9529594\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jaw clench against 26 face movements, wet ear electrodes (Knierim et al. 2022)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own detectors and gesture rules (MIT; <code>analysis/hands_free.py</code> and its line-by-line phone port); no machine-learning model.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "helmet": {"title": "The build-it-yourself helmet", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Planned. A home-built EEG helmet with 8, later 16, electrodes. Planned layout: a reference on the mid-forehead, both mastoids (the bones behind the ears) recorded as ordinary channels, and an 8-site core of M1 M2 F7 F8 Fz C3 C4 Pz. The app learns any layout from a short setup task.</p><h3>Evidence for this helmet: some</h3><p>Each site has evidence for its own job, but nobody has tested this exact mix; the layout is a planning assumption.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>A shared reference at the mid-forehead (Fpz) and recording both mastoids works better for sleep: lying on one side spoils one side of a headband, and a mastoid reference you lie on would spoil every channel.</li><li>The reference and bias electrodes need gel or hydrogel, not dry pins: a dry mastoid reference lost a whole night in a home study, and a bad forehead contact spoils every channel.</li><li>Distance matters more than exact position: layouts with a long side-to-side channel plus an eye channel reached near-lab sleep agreement (0.76-0.80), while forehead-only bands topped out at 0.37-0.45.</li><li>Forehead muscle tracked chin muscle closely during sleep (median correlation 0.98), so a chin electrode is optional.</li><li>Back-of-head electrodes scored worse in a 2025 headband, possibly from pillow contact, so the 8-site layout uses Pz (top-back) instead of O1/O2.</li><li>Fz (top front) earns its place for meditation: feedback there raised frontal-midline theta in a study with a fake-feedback control.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>Nobody has tested this exact 8- or 16-electrode layout.</li><li>Frontal &quot;mood asymmetry&quot; (left vs right forehead): only 13 of 270 re-analyses linked it to depression.</li><li>Sixteen channels are still too few for some standard cleaning methods or a true average reference.</li><li>Nothing here allows connecting electrodes to a person: the hardware&#x27;s own safety checks come first.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Each tracker learns its channels from a 1-2 minute eyes-closed and eyes-open setup task; positions are optional, and it re-calibrates when the electrodes move. Each helmet layout gets a version number.</li><li>A later 16-site layout keeps O1, O2, T7 and T8 (the headband&#x27;s sites), so months of headband data carry over.</li><li>The board keeps its own data format, which has error checks and a 32-bit sample counter.</li><li>Planned checks: compare the helmet with the headband on your own nights at the shared sites, and add a top-of-head motion sensor.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/human-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnhum.2018.00088/pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dreem headband layout lessons (Debellemaniere 2018)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.inb.uni-luebeck.de/fileadmin/files/PUBPDFS/NgMaBoMo13.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ngo 2013</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fnins.2020.00586/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Leach 2020: dry mastoid reference</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2104.04567\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ear-to-ear and mastoid layouts (Mikkelsen 2022)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://dovepress.com/comparison-of-emg-power-during-sleep-from-the-submental-and-frontalis--peer-reviewed-article-NSS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Forehead vs chin muscle (Levendowski 2018)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12561290\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dry 8-site band (Wang et al. 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/human-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnhum.2020.00246/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Frontal-midline theta feedback (Brandmeyer &amp; Delorme 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://elifesciences.org/articles/60595\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Frontal asymmetry reanalysis (Kolodziej 2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5859052/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Referencing with few electrodes</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pypi.org/project/pyriemann/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pyRiemann</a> (BSD-3): methods that cope with changing electrode sets.</li><li><a href=\"https://mne.tools/dev/auto_examples/decoding/ssd_spatial_filters.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MNE spatial filters (SSD)</a> (BSD-3): learn the best channel mix from a short setup task.</li><li>Open hardware to learn from: <a href=\"https://docs.openbci.com/AddOns/Headwear/MarkIV/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenBCI Ultracortex Mark IV</a> (GPL-3.0) and <a href=\"https://github.com/Cerelog-ESP-EEG/ESP-EEG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cerelog ESP-EEG</a> (firmware MIT, hardware CC BY-NC-SA).</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "jaw-and-blinks": {"title": "What the electrodes see (jaw, blinks, eye movements)", "grade": "strong", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>The side electrodes (T3, T4) sit on the main chewing muscle and the reference electrode sits on the forehead, so the headband sees jaw clenches, blinks and sideways glances clearly. The app uses them to stop false rewards, and later for night jaw counts and hands-free gestures. The setup test (20 blinks, 10 glances, 5 clenches, each on a tone) shows what your headband picks up.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: strong</h3><p>Jaw muscle at T3/T4 and blinks through the forehead reference are among the most reliable things this headband measures; eye direction, heart rate and breathing are not.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Jaw muscle at the temple electrodes can mimic brain rhythms across the head, so T3 and T4 are mainly a muscle sensor. A clench can fake &quot;focus&quot; or &quot;stress&quot;, and a lifted electrode can fake &quot;calm&quot;.</li><li>Blinks show on all four channels at once through the forehead reference. Blink rate depends on the task (about 11 a minute reading, 32 talking), so it is compared only with your own usual for the same task.</li><li>A sideways glance swings T3 and T4 in opposite directions, so T3 minus T4 doubles the eye signal and cancels blinks (roughly 2-3 µV per degree in studies with electrodes near the ears).</li><li>People clench far more by day than they think: 33.6 clench episodes an hour in temple-muscle recordings, and 23% of people who believed they did not clench did.</li><li>Dream sleep shows as bursts of eye movement: a Muse-S headband with similar temple sites matched a sleep lab at 0.76 agreement overall, with dream sleep and wake near perfect.</li><li>Dry electrodes at these side positions failed least of all sites in another headband and picked up left-right eye movements almost as well as gel electrodes (r = 0.92).</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>Where you are looking: on this exact headband, a study found eye jumps 90% of the time but told left from right only 62% of the time (50% is guessing).</li><li>A night clench count is not a bruxism (teeth grinding) diagnosis: muscle-only setups agreed with sleep-lab scoring below 0.1 on a 0-1 scale, while adding sound raised it to about 0.93. At most it is a reason to ask a dentist.</li><li>Jaw tension is muscle, not stress.</li><li>Heart rate from these electrodes is only an experiment (tested in 2 people); breathing, snoring and sleeping position: no study gets them from EEG electrodes.</li><li>The developer page says the signal stops at 70 Hz, but on the first recorded night muscle stayed 13-15 dB above quiet waking up to 124 Hz, so the app uses 55-120 Hz; the setup test&#x27;s clenches confirm it.</li><li>In sleep, eye-movement steps on T3-T4 came as often in deep sleep as in dream sleep on the first two recordings, so the app does not use them to find dream sleep.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Brain sound: jaw = 30-45 Hz power at T3/T4 (after removing 50 Hz mains hum) against its own resting level over the last 2 minutes; over about twice that level blocks the bells. The same rule at O1/O2 catches neck and scalp muscle. A blink is a slow swing at O1/O2 over 40 µV and 5 times its usual size.</li><li>Setup test: blinks, glances and clenches on tones in about 2 minutes, each scored against the quiet moments around it; &quot;visible&quot; at twice the background or more.</li><li>The shared detectors (built; docs/detectors.md): a blink is a dip of 50 µV or more on all four channels, 0.04-0.2 s wide, cancelled in T3-T4 (morning check-in: 3.1 a minute with eyes open, 0.5 with eyes closed); a sideways glance is a 40 µV step in T3-T4 (left and right wait for the setup test); jaw is 55-120 Hz at T3 and T4 over 4 times each side&#x27;s own level, both sides together (86 % of the first night&#x27;s loudest muscle seconds were caught, 7-8 % of the quietest); movement is three or more channels jumping past 200 µV at once.</li><li>Meditation and drifting off: two blinks in half a minute mean open eyes, so the drift-off detector does not call you asleep then.</li><li>The sleep report counts jaw-muscle bursts (both sides at once) and movements per hour asleep, shows the earlier nights&#x27; numbers beside them, and lists the night hour by hour. It is a trend to compare your own nights, never a bruxism (teeth grinding) result: swallowing, turning over or a pillow pressing on one side also light up the muscle band, and the limits are guesses until your setup test. On the first recorded night: about 23 bursts and 19 movements an hour asleep.</li><li>Planned: hands-free gestures (a double clench marks a moment, a long clench pauses the sound).</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.neurotechcenter.org/sites/default/files/misc/EMG%20contamination%20of%20EEG%20spectral%20and%20topographical%20characteristics.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Muscle in EEG (Goncharova 2003)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2902592\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Artefact guide (Kappenman and Luck 2010)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9927758/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Blink norms study</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5289990/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Blink detection rules (BLINKER)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2506.07193v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sideways glances on ear electrodes (King et al. 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/josnusd/66/1/66_23-0362/_article\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Daytime clenching (Yoshida et al. 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pure.etsmtl.ca/en/publications/assessing-the-performance-of-a-portable-electroencephalographic-s/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Muse-S sleep validation (Lanthier, Foti et al.)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.02592\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Earable: dry side electrodes</a></li><li><a href=\"https://fruct.org/files/publications/volume-36/fruct36/Rom.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eye direction on this headband (Romaniuk &amp; Kashevnik 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://europepmc.org/article/MED/31402440\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Muscle-only vs audio-video bruxism scoring (Miettinen 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2021.02.09.430184v1.full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Heart rate from EEG pins (brMEGA)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6856384/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Night movements in ear-EEG (Mikkelsen 2019)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://sdk.brainbit.com/device-recommendation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BrainBit developer page (forehead reference, 0-70 Hz)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own detectors (MIT); no machine-learning model.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/raphaelvallat/yasa/releases\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">YASA</a> (BSD-3): its dream-sleep eye-movement detector can take T3 and T4 as the left and right eye channels (planned for nights).</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "lucid-cues": {"title": "Lucid dream cues", "grade": "weak", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Built in the phone app (2026-11-19), not yet tried on a head. Off unless you turn it on. In the evening you practise in bed for about 15 minutes: a soft cue of three rising tones, each time paired with &quot;am I dreaming?&quot;. In the second half of the night, when the headband reads dream sleep (REM) with the eyes moving, the phone plays the same cue softly, hoping it reaches the dream and reminds you that you are dreaming. If it does, you can answer from inside the dream with your eyes: left-right-left-right. Some nights are silent on purpose, so after 12 nights the app can test whether the sound did anything for you.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: weak</h3><p>It worked in small sleep-lab studies with full sleep recording; the one controlled home test found no clear difference between cues and no cues, a larger lab replication found none either, and reading dream sleep from these four dry electrodes has not been checked.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>The method is called targeted lucidity reactivation (TLR). In the Swansea lab study (40 people, a morning nap after a normal night) a cue learned before sleep and played again in dream sleep was followed by a lucid dream proven by eye signals in 14 of 28 cued people against 2 of 12 uncued; the groups were not randomised between nap times.</li><li>Two-way talk with dreamers is possible: in four labs, 36 people tried; some answered questions from inside a lucid dream with agreed eye movements or face twitches (Konkoly 2021). The answer signal is left-right-left-right, &quot;large and at an even pace&quot;, easy to tell from ordinary dream-sleep eye movements (Konkoly 2026).</li><li>A home phone app (Konkoly 2024) played the cue 6 hours after the evening practice, starting at the hearing threshold and slowly louder, pausing on movement. Without a control group, lucid dreams rose from 0.74 to 2.11 a week (19 people). In the public test, nights with the trained cue were no different from nights with no cue at all; cues doubled movements in the night (0.19 against 0.10 per cue), and some users said the cues woke them like an alarm.</li><li>A preregistered three-lab replication (60 people, two naps each; a preprint) found proven lucid dreams in 38 % of naps with cues against 28 % with sham cues: no difference. Cues were heard in 71 % of cued dream periods and disturbed sleep in 24 %.</li><li>For comparison, without any device: in a study of 355 people, a memory technique with a short wake in the night gave a lucid dream on about 18-20 % of mornings against about 6 % before. Reviews call the method of intention the most effective; none found a technique that works reliably.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>Every positive result used lab sleep recording with eye and chin electrodes; this headband has neither. The live sleep model agreed with the full-night scoring on 89 % of epochs of one recorded night, but it was never compared with a sleep lab here, and dream sleep is the stage scoring without eye channels gets wrong most.</li><li>The sideways eye movements the temple electrodes see come in every stage on this headband, deep sleep included, so they cannot mark dream sleep on their own; the app only asks for them as a second sign (with the back of the head quiet, which kept them in dream sleep more than in deep sleep on one night).</li><li>The eye signal finder found no false signal in 6.5 hours of sleep of one night, but nobody has made a real signal on this headband yet, so whether it finds yours is unknown.</li><li>Risks: sounds in the night disturb sleep (twice the movements per cue at home; disturbed sleep in a quarter of cued periods in the lab). Students who tried often to induce lucid dreams reported more sleep problems and, over two months, more dissociation (feeling detached from yourself or the world); experiments on harm are lacking. Experts advise against lucid dream work with psychosis.</li><li>One person over a few weeks: the self-test will most likely say &quot;not clear&quot;, which is an honest answer, not a failure.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Off by default. The page says who should not use it: poor sleep, a sleep disorder, frequent nightmares, a history of psychosis or dissociation; and to stop if sleep gets worse or dreams and waking feel mixed up.</li><li>Evening practice (the home app&#x27;s): the loudness first (the cue every 3 s from very quiet, 2 dB louder each time; you tap when you hear it), then 4 cues with the words, 45 s apart, and 12 with eyes closed at 45-90 s gaps.</li><li>Night: only from 4.5 hours after Lights off; the live sleep model must read the two newest epochs as dream sleep (each at least 0.6 sure), no deep sleep in the 10 minutes before, an eye burst in the last 2 minutes, no movement in the last minute and no jaw muscle in the last 15 s, and the drift-off detector not saying awake. One cue every 30 s at most, 10 in a dream period, 30 a night; the first at your practice loudness, 1 dB louder each time up to +6 dB. A stir or a wake after a cue makes it 3 dB quieter and pauses 5 minutes; after 3 stirs, no more cues that night.</li><li>On the owner&#x27;s recorded night (no sound was played) the rule would have given 30 cue moments: 28 in what the full-night scoring calls dream sleep, 2 in light sleep at the end of a dream period, none in deep sleep or awake.</li><li>Morning: three questions (lucid? heard the sound? how much do you remember?), and a search of the night for left-right-left-right (3 even sweeps across) and, with a hands-free calibration, double jaw clenches.</li><li>The self-test: in each block of 4 nights 2 have sound, drawn by the phone before the first night and locked with a fingerprint; a silent night does everything the same without sound. After 12 nights it compares lucid mornings after nights with sound and silent nights, shuffling labels inside each block; stirs after cue moments are compared too, so the silent nights show how much the sound itself wakes you.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://cronfa.swan.ac.uk/Record/cronfa53997\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Carr et al., targeted lucidity reactivation in a morning nap (accepted manuscript)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10275878\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Konkoly et al. 2021, real-time dialogue with lucid dreamers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11542932\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Konkoly et al. 2024, a home phone app for lucid dream cues</a></li><li><a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/nc/article-pdf/2026/1/niaf067/66728394/niaf067.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Konkoly et al. 2026, puzzles solved in dreams with cues</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.06.21.600133v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Esfahani, Picard-Deland et al., three-lab cueing replication (preprint)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7379166/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The International Lucid Dream Induction Study</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36408823/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tan and Fan 2023, review of lucid dream induction</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.00384/pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Aviram and Soffer-Dudek 2018, lucid dream induction and well-being</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnins.2019.01423/epub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Soffer-Dudek 2020, are lucid dreams good for us?</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/dslaborg/AnySleep\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AnySleep</a> (MIT): the sleep model read live, its three runs exported to ONNX for the phone.</li><li><a href=\"https://pub.dev/packages/flutter_onnxruntime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_onnxruntime</a> (MIT) with ONNX Runtime (MIT): runs the model inside the night recorder.</li><li>Android&#x27;s own media player plays the cue; no sound library.</li><li>This project&#x27;s own cue rule, eye bursts and dream signal finder (MIT); the Python references are <code>analysis/lucid_cues.py</code> and <code>analysis/lucid_signals.py</code>. Two open TLR projects were read for the protocol, not used as code: <a href=\"https://github.com/rmallela26/TLR\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TLR for iPhone and Apple Watch</a> (MIT) and <a href=\"https://github.com/dreamento/dreamento\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dreamento</a> (MIT).</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "meditation-feedback": {"title": "Meditation feedback", "grade": "weak", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>During an eyes-closed meditation: sound plus a simple display of settled, restless or drifting toward sleep. At the end, 2 minutes without sound while the headband keeps measuring. Afterwards: a timeline of the session, the share of settled time, the longest settled stretch, movement and jaw events, alpha in the silent minutes, and from the 8th session whether that number is going up across sessions.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: weak</h3><p>Stillness, eyes-closed alpha and dozing are measurable here, but meditation headbands helped distress only a little and did not change the brain signal they trained, and there is no reliable &quot;meditation depth&quot; signal.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Consumer meditation headbands (Muse in 11 of 16 trials) gave a small drop in distress (g = -0.16, an effect size where 0.2 already counts as small) and no measurable change in the brain signal they trained. The practice itself seems to do the work.</li><li>Alpha (the 8-12 Hz wave that grows when you close your eyes) rose during sessions with real feedback, with replayed feedback and with none at all.</li><li>The best signs of learning, strongest first: a rise across sessions in short blocks without feedback; a rise in silent rest from before to after a session; a trend in the feedback blocks; a target that creeps up over weeks. Between 10% and 57% of people do not learn, depending on the definition.</li><li>Strategy lists do not help and effortful trying may hurt: people who learn say they &quot;sense&quot; the sound; people who do not say they &quot;will it&quot;.</li><li>Dozing works in opposite directions: with eyes closed, falling asleep removes alpha; with eyes open, sleepiness raises it, so &quot;more alpha is better&quot; can reward dozing.</li><li>Delay matters: in the one direct test, feedback 0.24 s late trained alpha best and left a lasting rise; at 0.74 s nothing lasting remained. Wired headphones and the phone&#x27;s speaker take 45 ms or less; classic Bluetooth headphones took 244-484 ms on phones (308-369 ms on average by codec), most of it inside the headset. LE Audio earbuds claim 30-80 ms, never measured independently on phones.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>A &quot;meditation depth&quot; or &quot;meditation score&quot;: no reliable signal for it exists at these electrode sites.</li><li>That the feedback makes you progress faster than the same practice without it.</li><li>A rising alpha line as proof that you are learning.</li><li>Frontal-midline theta (a 4-8 Hz rhythm at the top front of the head), which feedback raised in meditators in a fake-controlled study, sits where this headband has no electrode.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Built on the brain sound&#x27;s tested core: a silent baseline (30 s eyes open, 60 s eyes closed), the chosen 5, 10 or 20 minutes with sound, then 2 minutes without sound (the research&#x27;s no-feedback &quot;transfer&quot; block) while the headband keeps measuring, then the report.</li><li>Settled: eyes-closed alpha above your line with no muscle, blink or drowsiness flag. Restless: muscle, movement or blinks. Drifting: the shared drift-off detector.</li><li>The silent minutes&#x27; number: alpha on awake, clean seconds (at least 1 minute of them) against the silent eyes-closed minute before the sound. Its trend across sessions is the strongest sign of learning; against the minute before, it is also the second (silent rest after against before).</li><li>A learning verdict only after 8 sessions with the silent minutes (checked again at 12, then every 12 on the latest 12), and only if the trend&#x27;s 90% range stays above zero while muscle and drowsy time did not clearly rise with it. The trend is a Theil-Sen slope (the median of the slopes between every pair of sessions, so one odd session cannot swing it) with Sen&#x27;s 90% range. If it stays flat or falls, the app says so. It is &quot;seen together, not tested&quot;: practice itself, sleep or the headband&#x27;s fit can move it too. Only the replay check tests the sound: from its plans frozen on 2026-11-19 on, it also compares the silent minutes after live and replayed sound.</li><li>When the sound goes to Bluetooth headphones the screen says so in amber, before and during the session, and the session file keeps where the sound went and when it changed; you can still go on. Nothing measures the delay itself: the numbers above are the research&#x27;s.</li><li>The instructions say &quot;just notice&quot;, not &quot;try to relax&quot;.</li><li>Wording: &quot;a guided eyes-closed practice; practice, not treatment&quot;.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.jmir.org/2025/1/e68204\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Treves 2025: consumer meditation headbands</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10046\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Maaz 2025 preprint: alpha rises in all groups</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7327249/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Weber 2020: who does not learn</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-99235-7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Su 2021: learning indices</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7296848/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CRED-nf checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biopsycho.2023.108521\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Chikhi 2023: strategies</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6206258\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Davelaar 2018: sensing vs willing</a></li><li><a href=\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/905755554\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sleepiness and eyes-open alpha (Kaida 2006)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://personal.ntu.edu.sg/ctguan/Publications/C_2014_ZhangZhuo_IEEE_EMBC.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sleep onset removes alpha (Zhang 2014)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/human-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnhum.2020.00246/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Frontal-midline theta feedback in meditation (Brandmeyer &amp; Delorme 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://publications.hse.ru/mirror/pubs/share/direct/424296808.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Belinskaia 2020: feedback delay and alpha training</a></li><li><a href=\"https://developer.android.com/ndk/guides/audio/audio-latency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Android audio latency: 45 ms or less on the low-latency path</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.soundguys.com/android-bluetooth-latency-22732/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SoundGuys: Bluetooth latency on Android phones</a></li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/google/oboe/wiki/TechNote_BluetoothAudio\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Oboe: Bluetooth audio latency</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1968.10480934\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sen 1968: the slope&#x27;s range (Theil-Sen)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own feedback core (MIT), shared with the brain sound.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/mne-rt-org/mne-rt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MNE-RT</a> (BSD-3): open feedback protocols with no-feedback and sham blocks, used as a reference.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/alnitak/flutter_soloud\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_soloud</a> (MIT): the sound engine for the phone app.</li><li><a href=\"https://scipy.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SciPy</a> (BSD-3): its <code>theilslopes</code> is the reference the app&#x27;s trend is checked against.</li><li>Android&#x27;s AudioManager (part of Android): where the sound goes, for the Bluetooth note.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "morning-check-in": {"title": "Morning check-in", "grade": "strong", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>About 5 minutes each morning: a contact check, 2 minutes with eyes closed, 2 minutes looking at a dot, then three quick ratings (sleepiness 1-9, mood, tension). It measures your alpha rhythm (the 8-12 Hz wave at the back of the head that grows when you close your eyes) and compares it only with your own earlier mornings.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: strong</h3><p>Eyes-closed alpha at the back of the head is one of the most reliable EEG measures and this headband&#x27;s back electrodes match lab electrodes; what a day-to-day change means for your day is less certain.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>A dry headband with exactly this layout matched lab gel electrodes for back-of-head alpha: correlation 0.90.</li><li>Averaged over minutes, alpha strength and alpha speed (the exact rate of your alpha peak) are very repeatable: ICC about 0.84-0.87 over 5 years in lab caps (ICC: a repeatability score where 1 is perfect).</li><li>From second to second, alpha speed jumps 1-2 Hz, so only minutes of eyes-closed data give a usable value.</li><li>Ratios (such as eyes-closed against eyes-open alpha) repeat better month to month than single measures.</li><li>Habits move these numbers: caffeine lowers alpha and speeds the peak, binge drinking slowed alpha speed by about 0.7 Hz, and sleeping pills and antidepressants lower alpha strength.</li><li>The 1-9 sleepiness rating (Karolinska scale) is the standard short rating; EEG signs of sleepiness follow it on average, and the rating is about as sensitive as the EEG.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>Nobody has published how repeatable these numbers are on a dry headband like this one; all the figures above come from wet lab caps.</li><li>&quot;Alpha slows when you are tired&quot; is not supported: in 77 teenagers alpha speed rose after short nights. So the check-in does not read tiredness from alpha speed.</li><li>About one person in ten shows little alpha when closing the eyes; for them these numbers carry less.</li><li>The spectrum slope (how fast power falls with frequency) is less repeatable (ICC 0.54-0.62).</li><li>Three minutes with eyes closed is better supported than two; the length is still open.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Contact check, then 2 min eyes closed, then 2 min eyes open on a dot, with soft tones at each change; then the ratings. Each change is marked in the recording. The phone app runs the same routine itself, between nights (the main screen&#x27;s Morning section), and gives the same numbers as the laptop on the same signal. As the phases count seconds of signal, a tone you do not hear leaves your eyes closed in the eyes-open phase: Start first checks the media volume and Bluetooth headphones, and a phone call or another app&#x27;s sound stops the check-in.</li><li>Only clean data counts: the signal is cut into 2-second pieces, and a piece is dropped if it swings over 150 µV, is flat, or is far noisier than the rest of the phase. The first 3 s and last 1 s of each phase are skipped, and each channel needs 20 s of clean data per phase.</li><li>Alpha speed is the centre of the power that rises above the smooth background curve in 7-14 Hz, eyes closed; it stays empty when there is no clear peak.</li><li>The laptop compares each morning with the middle value and range of your last 14 check-ins: &quot;within / above / below your range&quot;. A note says so until 5 exist. Never a score, never a norm from other people.</li><li>On the phone the ratings are followed by quick taps: cups of coffee or strong tea the day before (and whether one came in the 8 hours before sleep), drinks of alcohol, caffeine before the check-in; then an optional 1-minute reaction test (the median of your taps). The numbers go to the trends page, compared with your own usual.</li><li>Planned: a medication tap, and a fresh baseline when medication or hardware changes.</li><li>The laptop page also has a 2-minute setup test (blinks, glances, jaw clenches); see &quot;What the electrodes see&quot;.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11679099/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mikhaylov 2024: dry headband vs gel, same layout</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13456336/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cerebral Cortex 2026: 5-year repeatability</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.eneuro.org/lookup/doi/10.1523/ENEURO.0118-25.2025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">eNeuro 2025: alpha speed jumps second to second</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12589806/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Test-retest 2025: ratios repeat better</a></li><li><a href=\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/868660341\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Caffeine and alpha (Barry 2005)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5995627\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Binge drinking and alpha speed</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10049386/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Caffeine and alpha speed (2023)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/905755554\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sleepiness ratings vs EEG (Kaida 2006)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9154075/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Campbell 2022: alpha speed rose after short nights</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537023/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Alpha basics (StatPearls)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10548-024-01067-x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Brain Topography 2024: 3 minutes</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pypi.org/project/numpy/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">numpy</a> and <a href=\"https://pypi.org/project/scipy/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">scipy</a> (BSD-3): the spectra and filters in the laptop analysis.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/holgern/pyedflib/blob/master/LICENSE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pyedflib</a> (BSD-3): reads the recording.</li><li>No model and no outside library in the page itself.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "nap-coach": {"title": "Nap coach", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Built in the phone app (2026-11-19), not yet tried on a head. You pick a length (20, 30, 45 or 90 minutes) and tap Lights off. The app watches your sleep live and wakes you with a soft rising sound before deep sleep starts, or when your length is up, whichever comes first. Afterwards it shows how long you took to fall asleep, the stages and why it woke you.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Short naps that end before deep sleep leave less grogginess in a small, careful study, and the sleep model reads deep sleep from these electrodes; but the live stages have only been compared with the same model on one recorded night, never with a sleep lab, and no study has tested an EEG-timed nap alarm.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>In 24 young people after a short night, a nap of 10 minutes of real sleep helped at once and for up to 2.5 hours; naps of 20 and 30 minutes of sleep left them groggy (sleep inertia: the slow, foggy minutes after waking) at first, and their gains came later and were no larger.</li><li>A review of sleep inertia: grogginess usually clears in 15-30 minutes, full recovery can take an hour; it is less likely after naps of 30 minutes or less, but not ruled out. Waking from deep sleep has been linked to worse performance in some studies, though newer work is less clear.</li><li>A review of 54 nap studies found naps help memory and alertness (alertness d = 0.61: a medium-to-large effect), whatever the nap&#x27;s length.</li><li>Scoring sleep live, with only the past, costs only 1-2 accuracy points against scoring it afterwards in two studies (78.5 % live vs 80.7 % in one; one back-of-the-head channel scored about 5 points below a forehead one).</li><li>Deep-sleep waves are biggest at the front of the head. Every channel of this headband is measured against a forehead electrode, so the waves reach all four; on one recorded night the back channels told deep sleep from the other stages well.</li><li>Commercial headbands already do this: Dreem found 70 % of deep sleep live; Muse wakes you at &quot;the shallowest moment&quot; of your last cycle, with only its own, unpublished data behind it.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>No study has shown that an EEG-timed nap or alarm makes you less groggy than a plain timer. A bedroom alarm with light, sound and temperature did no better than a normal one.</li><li>The live stages are AnySleep, the same sleep model as the morning report, read 2 minutes behind the newest data. On one recorded night they agreed with the model&#x27;s own full-night scoring on about 9 in 10 of the 30-second stretches; that shows the live version is consistent with the full one, not that either is right on this headband.</li><li>Live mistakes lean to calling light sleep &quot;deep&quot;, which wakes you a little early; deep sleep missed for a few minutes is the rarer mistake.</li><li>Light sleep (N1) is poorly scored everywhere; the &quot;fell asleep&quot; time comes from the alpha rule instead, checked on only two recorded sleep onsets so far.</li><li>How fast and how battery-hungry the model is on your phone has not been measured yet.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Its own screen between nights, like the dream catcher; it holds the headband while it runs, and the screen stays on, black and dim. You may lock the phone: a notification with Stop keeps the nap going. Lights off first checks the phone&#x27;s media volume and whether the sound goes to Bluetooth headphones, and says so: the wake sound is the nap&#x27;s only way to wake you.</li><li>Lights off starts three things: the nap&#x27;s EEG recording (part of that day&#x27;s sleep report later), the alpha rule for &quot;fell asleep&quot; (your first 60 eyes-closed seconds are the starting level), and the live sleep model.</li><li>The live sleep model: every 30 seconds, from 5 minutes in, AnySleep scores the last 60 minutes of signal prepared the way a phone can (only the past) and keeps the stage of the stretch 2 minutes back. Two such stretches of deep sleep in a row wake you: about 3 minutes into deep sleep, before it settles.</li><li>The length counts from Lights off and always ends the nap, so the nap works on the timer alone if the model is too slow (it times every check: two in a row over 15 seconds drop it to 1 of its 3 runs, then to the timer), does not answer within 60 seconds, fails, or the signal is lost. The screen and the report say which; a nap the timer alone ran shows its stages as &quot;not scored&quot;, and the last 2 minutes and the wake are never scored.</li><li>The wake sound is three soft rising notes, a little louder every 20 seconds until you tap &quot;I&#x27;m awake&quot; (5 minutes at most).</li><li>The report: when you fell asleep, minutes of light, deep and dream sleep, why it woke you, how long you took to tap, and how fast the model was.</li><li>Not built: a coffee-nap reminder, a &quot;10 minutes of real sleep&quot; length counted from falling asleep, and the smart alarm inside the night recorder.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16796222/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Brooks &amp; Lack 2006: which nap length is best</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.dovepress.com/sleep-inertia-current-insights-peer-reviewed-fulltext-article-NSS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hilditch &amp; McHill 2019: sleep inertia review</a></li><li><a href=\"https://medicine.nus.edu.sg/newsletters/issue45/science-of-life/how-naps-can-be-beneficial-for-you\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Leong 2022 nap meta-analysis (summary)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.06.12.597764v1.full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Esparza-Iaizzo: live vs afterwards scoring</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10017438/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GSSC: past-only scoring 1-2 % below</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6729597/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Massimini 2004: where deep-sleep waves start</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5853451/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Debellemaniere 2018: Dreem&#x27;s live deep-sleep detection</a></li><li><a href=\"https://choosemuse.com/blogs/news/smart-wakeup-how-muse-wakes-you-when-your-brain-is-ready\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Muse Smart Wakeup</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10969141\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Campanella 2024: a smart bedroom alarm did no better</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.14461v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AnySleep preprint</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/dslaborg/AnySleep\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AnySleep</a> (MIT): the sleep model, its three runs exported to ONNX for the phone.</li><li><a href=\"https://pub.dev/packages/flutter_onnxruntime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_onnxruntime</a> (MIT) with ONNX Runtime (MIT): runs the model on the phone.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/alnitak/flutter_soloud\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_soloud</a> (MIT): plays the wake sound.</li><li>This project&#x27;s own live preparation and drift-off detector (MIT); the Python reference is <code>analysis/live_stager.py</code>.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "noise-cleaning": {"title": "Noise flags and cleaning", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Marks each stretch of signal as good, degraded or missing (lost contact, a flat line, huge swings, muscle noise), keeps bad stretches out of the numbers, and plans to clean only the flagged stretches, lightly. The raw recording is always kept unchanged.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Simple rules for size, flatness and muscle noise are standard, and the headband&#x27;s maker publishes its own thresholds; no 2025-2026 learned cleaner has been tested on a four-electrode dry headband.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>BrainBit&#x27;s own software flags artefacts (noise from muscles, movement or contact) with published thresholds: 110 µV, a limit on fast-wave power, and an alert after 4 seconds. It flags but does not clean.</li><li>ZUNA (2026), the best-known new &quot;cleaning&quot; model, is a 380-million-parameter generator that redraws the stretches you mark as bad from the other channels. Its papers tested only caps with 32 or more electrodes, and its makers say the output is imputed (guessed) data, not measurement.</li><li>GEDAI, another 2026 cleaner, was the only one that helped a real task in a nine-method benchmark, but version 1.5 was caught cutting alpha by 77.5% in a dirty recording while its own quality score missed it.</li><li>Cleaning less works better: SPAR-EEG treats only artefact-heavy stretches with two classical tools and made alpha clearer in dry-electrode recordings during exercise.</li><li>A learned denoiser tuned to rebuild the signal lowered a later classifier&#x27;s accuracy (0.547 against 0.612 on the raw data); only 3 of 199 single-channel methods were ever checked for leaving clean signal usable.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>No learned cleaner (ZUNA, GEDAI, iPSD) has been tested on four dry electrodes; the app does not use them on your data.</li><li>A cleaned stretch is a repair, not a measurement, and a stretch redrawn by a generator is a guess.</li><li>The thresholds are planning values until real recordings with this headband recalibrate them.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Morning check-in: 2-second pieces are dropped if they swing over 150 µV after filtering to 1-40 Hz, are flat (under 0.5 µV), or are three times noisier than the rest of the phase.</li><li>Sleep scoring: a 30-second stretch missing over 5 s is not scored; missing data never becomes &quot;awake&quot; or &quot;deep&quot;.</li><li>Brain sound: a channel is unusable with no contact, a flat line, or a swing over 300 µV within 1 s; muscle and blink checks block rewards.</li><li>Planned default for every feature: remove slow drift and 50 Hz mains hum, flag each channel-second, clean only flagged spans with ATAR (a wavelet method) or a simple rebuild, keep flagged stretches out of trend numbers, keep the raw file.</li><li>Any cleaner must pass one test before use: on clean stretches, eyes-closed alpha at O1/O2 moves less than about 0.5 dB and the eyes-closed to eyes-open ratio stays within about 10%, while jaw-clench noise at T3/T4 falls. ZUNA and GEDAI are only compared on the laptop.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://sdk.brainbit.com/lib-emotions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BrainBit SDK: artefact rules</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.18478v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ZUNA paper</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.27308v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ZUNA1.1 paper</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-11118324/v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ARQA audit of GEDAI</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31195\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nine-method cleaning benchmark (Hajhassani 2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/tnsre.2026.3734253\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SPAR-EEG: clean only the bad stretches</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.08594\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ultra-compact denoisers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1088/1741-2552/aeb0ce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Scoping review of single-channel cleaning</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2605.06724v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">iPSD v2</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own flagging rules (MIT).</li><li><a href=\"https://spkit.github.io/modules/generated/spkit.eeg.ATAR.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">spkit ATAR</a> (MIT): wavelet cleaning of flagged stretches (planned).</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/nbara/python-meegkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">meegkit</a> (BSD-3): mains-hum removal and other classical cleaning.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/Zyphra/zuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ZUNA</a> (Apache-2.0): laptop comparison only.</li><li><a href=\"https://pypi.org/project/pygedai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pygedai</a> (non-commercial licence): laptop comparison only.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "replay-check": {"title": "Replay check (does the live link matter?)", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet run on a phone). When switched on (it is off by default), some meditation or alpha-theta sessions secretly replay the sound of one of your own earlier sessions instead of following your brain live. After every 12 sessions, the app compares live with replayed sessions: the trained brain signal, how you felt, and your guesses about which was which.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>It copies the fake-feedback controls used in good trials, and replays of people&#x27;s own sound went unnoticed in one study; but whole-session replays can be partly sensed, and one person needs many sessions.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Replaying a person&#x27;s own earlier sound is &quot;a very stringent control&quot;: short replays mixed into live sessions went unnoticed by all 18 people reached afterwards.</li><li>Whole-session replays felt less controllable than live ones in two of three crossover studies, so expect that you will sometimes guess right.</li><li>Alpha (the wave that grows when you close your eyes) rose in every group of a double-blind study (neither the people nor the staff knew who got what): real, replayed and passive watching. Only live minus replay can show that the live link matters.</li><li>In the own-replay study, live beat replay in only 6-11 of 24 people, so &quot;no difference&quot; for one person is a likely and legitimate result.</li><li>Good trials compare against a fake that looks the same; a research checklist notes that few feedback programs are designed for this kind of blinding.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>It tests one thing: whether, within a session, the live link changes your signal or experience more than an identical-sounding replay. It does not test long-term learning or any health benefit.</li><li>Sessions needed (the research report&#x27;s own estimate): telling live from replay takes about 13-37 sessions; seeing your EEG follow live sessions at a large effect about 34; a small effect about 128, which is out of reach for one person.</li><li>Twelve sessions is only a pilot; plan 24-36 randomised sessions.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Off by default: Training settings, from the gear button on the meditation and alpha-theta screens. While on, the same reminder appears before every session: &quot;Replay check is on. This session&#x27;s sound may follow your brain live or replay one of your earlier sessions. Practise as usual; afterwards you&#x27;ll be asked to guess.&quot;</li><li>Each kind of session (meditation, alpha-theta) has its own check. It first collects 4 live sessions of that kind with a saved log (sessions from before you switched it on count), so there is something to replay.</li><li>What is replayed: the sound settings and bells (or chimes and gongs) logged in your own earlier live sessions of the same kind, never the raw EEG. The app picks from the last 10 with a reward rate close to their usual, the least replayed first, and joins two when one is shorter than the session.</li><li>Jaw tension always roughens the sound live, and the muscle, blink, signal and sleep checks act the same way in both kinds of session: the sound goes neutral and no bell sounds until the check has been clear as long as a live bell needs. So a jaw clench cannot give a replay away.</li><li>Sessions are drawn in shuffled blocks of 4 (2 live, 2 replay) from a random secret made on the phone. Before the first such session the plan (what is measured, how many sessions, which sessions are left out, the analysis) and the SHA-256 fingerprint of the hidden order are saved, and they never change; Training settings shows the date and the start of the fingerprint. No bell count and no &quot;settled&quot; word are shown while the check is on.</li><li>After each check session, before the report: how calm you feel, your guess (live, replay or not sure, and how sure), &quot;how in control did you feel?&quot; and &quot;how random did the sound seem?&quot;.</li><li>The measure: meditation, your alpha during the sound against the same session&#x27;s silent eyes-closed minute; alpha-theta, the change in theta against alpha; both on awake, clean seconds only. A session with over 30 % of its time asleep, restless or without signal is shown but left out of the test.</li><li>Results only for whole sets of 12 sessions, never one session: live minus replay with a test that reshuffles the labels the way the draw could have fallen, your guesses against chance, how the sessions felt, every session of the set, and a check that the plan and the order are unchanged. Plan 24 to 36; every session is reported, and the check never stops early on a good result.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.eneuro.org/lookup/doi/10.1523/ENEURO.0498-18.2019\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Biswas &amp; Ray 2019: own-replay control</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41391628/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">de Matos 2026: replay can be sensed</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10046\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Maaz 2025 preprint: double-blind, alpha rises in all groups</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/psyp.70285\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Maaz 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32853703/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ICAN 2021: ADHD trial with replayed feedback</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7296848/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CRED-nf checklist</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own session log and exact replay (MIT), tested with the brain sound.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/mne-rt-org/mne-rt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MNE-RT</a> (BSD-3): has sham and replay protocols, used as a reference. No tool found offers a blinded replay of your own sessions, so that part is built here.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "self-experiments": {"title": "Self-experiments (does this habit change that number, for you?)", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet run on a phone). You pick one habit (&quot;no screens in the last hour before bed&quot;) and one number (time asleep, morning sleepiness, reaction time...). The app draws random days for 4 to 8 weeks: on some you do it, on the others you don&#x27;t. At the planned end it tells you the difference, with a range, in one sentence.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Random days are the accepted way to learn what causes what in one person, and the test used needs no assumptions; but you know which kind of day it is, the numbers are noisy, and a few weeks can only show large effects.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Randomised experiments on one person (alternating or random days or blocks) beat any &quot;seen together&quot; link for saying what causes what: when chance picks the days, nothing else can explain the difference.</li><li>Apps that only compare tagged days with untagged days cannot do this. One asks for just 5 days with and 5 without, another for 3 and 3, and both admit that other things differing on those days distort the result.</li><li>Published one-person trials are often analysed badly: in a review, 83.8 % ignored that one day resembles the next, and 99.1 % gave no range for the effect. They are also short: a median of 20 measurements and 3 switches.</li><li>Days needed, as a rough rule from the research notes: about 31 days divided by the squared effect (in units of your own day-to-day spread). So about a month for a large effect (one whole spread), about four months for a medium one.</li><li>People can finish such experiments when an app sets them up: 15 people completed 12-day food experiments with a wizard and reminders, and wanted room for days when life got in the way. Sleep apps have guided people through short experiments in the same way.</li><li>When people do not keep to the plan, counting days as they were drawn stays fair; counting them by what was done lets the old confusions back in. Statisticians report both.</li><li>A habit worth testing first: late caffeine. In trials it cut sleep by 45 minutes on average, and 400 mg six hours before bed cut measured sleep by 1.1 to 1.2 hours while sleep diaries noticed 41 minutes.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>You are not blinded: you know today&#x27;s task, and expecting an effect can move ratings (sleepiness, mood, tension) by itself. Measured numbers (time asleep, reaction time) are less open to this. One writer who ran blinded sleep experiments for years calls an unblinded self-experiment &quot;less than n = 1&quot;.</li><li>Four weeks finds only large effects. &quot;Not clear yet&quot; is the most likely answer for a small one, and it does not mean &quot;no effect&quot;.</li><li>The answer is about you, in these weeks. It says nothing about other people, or about other seasons of your life.</li><li>One experiment can be wrong by chance about 1 time in 20 even when nothing is there. Run it again before you rely on it.</li><li>An effect that lasts longer than a day (a habit whose effect carries over) blurs the two kinds of day together and makes the difference look smaller than it is.</li><li>The sleep numbers come from a sleep model that is a first guess on this headband; the experiment can only be as good as the number it tests.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Trends, then Experiments. One experiment at a time. You choose a question (four ready-made ones, or your own &quot;do&quot; against &quot;don&#x27;t&quot;), ONE number from the trends page, and 4, 6 or 8 weeks. Day 1 is the next day. Nothing can be changed after the start.</li><li>The order of days comes from a random secret made on the phone, in shuffled blocks of 4 days (2 of each kind), drawn again until every weekday has both kinds. The plan and the SHA-256 fingerprint of the hidden order are saved before day 1; the page shows the fingerprint&#x27;s start and checks at the end that nothing changed.</li><li>Each day the page (and one line on the main screen and after the morning check-in) shows that day&#x27;s task. The days ahead stay hidden. A day runs from 04:00 to 04:00.</li><li>The next day it asks &quot;did you keep to it?&quot;. Days you did not keep still count as they were drawn; the same difference by what you did is shown beside it, labelled &quot;seen together&quot;.</li><li>A task&#x27;s number is the next day&#x27;s: the night you wake from and that morning&#x27;s check-in. A day without the number is left out.</li><li>The answer opens the day after the last number. &quot;Look early anyway&quot; asks first and stays marked on the final answer; &quot;Stop&quot; keeps the days that are over and is marked too.</li><li>The test: the difference of the two means, compared with the same difference under every order of days the draw could have given (all of them up to 300 000, else 50 000 random ones). The range is every size of effect that test does not rule out at 5 %.</li><li>The words: &quot;likely real for you&quot; when chance alone would give such a difference in fewer than 1 of 20 draws; &quot;little or no effect for you&quot; when the whole range is smaller than what would matter (planning values: 30 minutes of sleep, 1 point of a rating, 5 % of reaction time, a quarter of the jaw bursts, 0.2 Hz of alpha speed, a fifth of alpha strength or the eyes-closed rise); &quot;not clear yet&quot; otherwise; &quot;too few days&quot; under 5 days of each kind.</li><li>Only the one chosen number is tested. Every other number is shown as a plain difference under &quot;seen together, not tested&quot;.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2112.13991v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liao 2021: review of one-person trials, autocorrelation ignored</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6955665/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Simulation paper citing Gabler 2011: typical designs</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12934031/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Non-compliance in one-person trials (ISTOP)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.03423\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Daza: person as population</a></li><li><a href=\"https://people.cs.umass.edu/~rkarkar/pubs/conf-chi2017-tummytrials.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TummyTrials, CHI 2017</a></li><li><a href=\"https://jeffhuang.com/papers/SleepBandits_CHI20.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SleepBandits, CHI 2020</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.whoop.com/thelocker/monthly-performance-assessment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Whoop: monthly performance assessment</a></li><li><a href=\"https://bearable.app/support/troubleshooting/my-correlations-are-wrong-dont-make-sense/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bearable: why correlations can be wrong</a></li><li><a href=\"https://gwern.net/Zeo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gwern: Zeo sleep self-experiments</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.lesswrong.com/lw/c5f/case_study_testing_confirmation_bias\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">LessWrong: confirmation bias case study</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36870101/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gardiner 2023: caffeine and sleep, pooled</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3805807/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Drake 2013: caffeine 0, 3 or 6 hours before bed</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own randomisation, frozen plan and randomisation test (MIT), shared with the replay check.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/studyu-health/studyu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">StudyU</a> (MIT): an open platform for one-person trials, used as a reference for the design; not bundled.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "sharp-hours": {"title": "Sharp hours (when in the day are you sharpest?)", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet run on a phone), and only when you switch it on: the assistant&#x27;s proposal, not a feature you asked for. A few times a day, at random moments while you are awake, a notification asks &quot;How sharp do you feel right now?&quot; (one tap, 1 to 5). Two of the day&#x27;s questions also suggest a 1-minute reaction test. After 3 weeks the Sharp hours page shows your answers by the time of day, by hours since you woke and by the sleep-pressure estimate.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>It does not use the headband at all: it rests on your own ratings and a tap test, which field studies show change with the time of day and with sleep. How much they change for you, and whether your ratings change at all, only your own weeks can show.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>The best-tested measure of &quot;how good was this hour&quot; is still a quick self-rating. In 81 software developers, computer app-time logs explained only about 9 % of how productive a day felt; a personal baseline and a few day facts (slept well, interruptions, travel) took it to 47 %.</li><li>Logs that do work count focus sessions, not hours: a measure of runs of related work lasting 35-60 minutes matched what engineers wrote in diaries; total hours in focus did not.</li><li>Self-rated productivity hardly follows the clock: in 21 developers rating every hour for 2-3 weeks, morning against afternoon explained 0.66 % of the ratings, mood 12 %. Another study of 20 developers found only rough, &quot;not very pronounced&quot; daily patterns after about 11 days each.</li><li>Speed follows the clock more clearly: in 31,793 people, typing speed varied by 23 % with the time of day and 19 % with the time since waking up, and was lowest near the usual sleep time. Phone reaction tests in daily life swung by about 30 % in 20 people over 40 days.</li><li>Two nights in a row under 6 hours went with slower performance for about six days.</li><li>&quot;Morning person or night owl&quot; predicts little: over 80 % of 65 studies found no main effect of chronotype on thinking, and a 446-person study found no general boost from testing at your preferred time.</li><li>An afternoon dip in performance is real and can happen even without lunch.</li><li>The shortest reaction test validated for sleep loss is 3 minutes (the PVT-B); a 1-minute test is noisier, so the app uses many of them over weeks rather than trusting one.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>A clear best hour from ratings may never appear: the hour explains very little of how sharp people rate themselves. &quot;No clear best time&quot; is a likely and honest answer.</li><li>What you do at an hour shapes the answer: meetings, meals, coffee, the kind of work you leave for the afternoon. The page shows patterns seen together, never a cause.</li><li>Three weeks is a planning value for a first rough look; a dependable hour-by-hour curve needs months.</li><li>You rate how you feel, and feeling can disagree with doing: developers in one trial believed an AI tool made them 20 % faster while it made them 19 % slower.</li><li>Answers you give only when it suits you lean the pattern: that is why the questions come at random moments, and a rating from the page is marked as such.</li><li>Not tested on anyone yet, including you. The phone&#x27;s delivery of the questions (HyperOS can hold notifications back) is checked by the page itself, not proven.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Off until you switch it on (Trends, then Sharp hours). The page says it is the assistant&#x27;s idea, and it can be switched off or deleted there.</li><li>Questions: 4 a day (or 3 or 6) inside your waking hours (suggested from your recorded nights: from 30 minutes after your usual wake-up to an hour before your usual sleep; else 10:00 to 22:00). The waking hours are cut into equal parts and each part gets one random minute, at least an hour apart, so each day has early and late answers to compare and no hour is asked more than another over the weeks. Two a day suggest the reaction test.</li><li>Never during a night recording, a sound session or a session on screen, and never in the quiet hours. A question that arrives over an hour late is dropped; one you miss disappears after an hour; &quot;In 15 min&quot; puts it off once.</li><li>One tap saves &quot;how sharp&quot; on 1 Foggy, 2 Slow, 3 Okay, 4 Sharp, 5 Very sharp, at the time you answer. The reaction test is the app&#x27;s 1-minute test: a dot after 2-5 seconds, tap fast; the median of at least 5 taps.</li><li>Collecting: for the first 3 weeks and about 60 answers the page shows only the dots and how far it is from a first pattern. Reaction times need 30 tests as well.</li><li>Then, by 2-hour blocks of the clock, of hours since waking, or of sleep pressure: each block&#x27;s mean with a range that holds 80 % of means when whole days are drawn again at random. A best time is named only when the same block (or it and its neighbour together) beats every other block in 9 of 10 of those redraws and is half a point better (5 % quicker for reaction time). Everything is labelled &quot;seen together, not tested&quot;.</li><li>Trends shows the day&#x27;s mean sharpness and the middle of the day&#x27;s reaction tests; self-experiments can test either as the next day&#x27;s number.</li><li>Computer focus sessions (ActivityWatch) belong to the laptop and are not part of this yet.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07428\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Beller 2020: measured against self-reported productivity</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.04711\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Brown 2023: focus sessions from logs</a></li><li><a href=\"https://aserebre.win.tue.nl/TSE2021.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Girardi 2021: emotions and perceived productivity of developers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://Www.Gwern.net/doc/psychology/writing/2017-meyer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Meyer 2017: the work life of developers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.07083\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Althoff 2017: sleep, time of day and performance in 31,793 people</a></li><li><a href=\"https://depts.washington.edu/chilllab/?p=405\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Abdullah 2016: cognitive rhythms with a phone reaction test</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.15583\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Visuri 2025: phone reaction tests and sleep over two months</a></li><li><a href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0239984\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">de Jong 2020: typing slows with time on task</a></li><li><a href=\"https://bura.brunel.ac.uk/bitstream/2438/31067/6/FullText.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Chauhan 2025: chronotype and cognition, 65 studies</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1525/collabra.88337\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Rey-Mermet 2023: no general synchrony boost</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15892914/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Monk 2005: the post-lunch dip</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3197786\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Basner 2011: the 3-minute PVT-B</a></li><li><a href=\"https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">METR 2025: believed faster, measured slower</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own sampling schedule, resampling ranges and reaction test (MIT); Android&#x27;s alarm and notification services, no extra library.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "sleep-recording": {"title": "Sleep recording", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Records the headband&#x27;s four channels all night to a file (on the laptop now, on the phone soon), keeps going through Bluetooth drops, and shows a live check of each electrode&#x27;s contact and noise.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>The data stream is tiny and the back electrodes match lab electrodes well, but nobody has published how often a dry headband like this keeps good contact through a whole night at home.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>A dry headband with exactly this electrode layout matched lab gel electrodes for the alpha rhythm (the 8-12 Hz wave at the back of the head that grows when you close your eyes): correlation 0.90 in 19 adults.</li><li>The headband sends only about 3,000 bytes a second, so a phone or laptop keeps up easily. An 8-hour night is about 90 MB as a BDF+ file (a standard EEG file format), about 33 GB a year.</li><li>Dry headbands lose some nights at home: about 10% of dry home nights failed in one study, and a Muse-S study lost 16% of nights to poor signal.</li><li>Bluetooth drops come from a weak radio path (distance, your body, a pillow), not from a slow link. Keep the phone within about 1 m, in line of sight, not under the pillow.</li><li>Brain recordings identify a person like a fingerprint: two channels of home sleep EEG re-identified 86% of 35 people. That is why recordings stay on your own devices.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>No study has tested whether this headband is comfortable and keeps contact while you lie on it. Whether it does decides whether the night features work at all.</li><li>No gap-free night is promised yet: the phone app&#x27;s all-night test has not been run, and some phone makers (Xiaomi among the strictest) stop apps running in the background.</li><li>The headband&#x27;s battery reading sticks at 0-2% or 100% while streaming, and makers&#x27; figures differ (10 to 15 hours), so &quot;hours left&quot; is an estimate from the trend.</li><li>The headband&#x27;s own packet counter sometimes repeats numbers. That is a known bug in the vendor&#x27;s software; the app can count it but not fix it.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>One program, the recorder, holds the headband&#x27;s single Bluetooth link, writes every sample to disk and shares a live stream with any page or tool. A page closing never stops the recording.</li><li>After 40 s without data the laptop recorder starts a fresh process and a new file. In the morning the night&#x27;s files are joined into one BDF+ file; missing time is marked as a gap, never filled with made-up values.</li><li>The live check page shows, per electrode: contact, noise, mains hum and a moving trace, plus an eyes-closed alpha test for the back pair.</li><li>Battery: stuck readings are ignored and a line through the last 90 minutes gives &quot;about N h left&quot;.</li><li>Phone app (in progress): one background service of Android&#x27;s &quot;connected device&quot; type, which has no time limit, reconnecting straight to the saved headband instead of scanning for it.</li><li>The same service watches for falling asleep (the drift-off detector, back-of-head alpha fading for 3 minutes) on the samples it already receives, after they are saved; if the detector ever fails or takes too long it switches itself off and the recording goes on. <strong>Lights off</strong> on the night screen marks the moment you close your eyes, and the morning report compares the detector with the sleep model.</li><li>Nothing is uploaded. There is no account and no server.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11679099/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mikhaylov 2024: dry headband vs gel, same layout</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.06.01.26354591v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ZMax home nights (about 10% failed)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://espace2.etsmtl.ca/33349/1/Lina-JM-2026-33349.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Muse-S sleep study (Lanthier 2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.bluetooth.com/learn-about-bluetooth/key-attributes/range/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bluetooth range</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.20454\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Two-channel sleep EEG re-identification</a></li><li><a href=\"https://dontkillmyapp.com/xiaomi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">dontkillmyapp: Xiaomi</a></li><li><a href=\"https://sdk.brainbit.com/device-brainbit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BrainBit SDK device page (battery up to 10 h)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://gitlab.com/brainbit-inc/brainbit-sdk/-/issues/45\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BrainBit issue 45: packet numbers repeat</a></li><li><a href=\"https://developer.android.com/develop/background-work/services/fgs/service-types\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Android foreground service types</a></li><li><a href=\"https://biosemi.com/faq/file_format.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BioSemi BDF format FAQ</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pub.dev/packages/neurosdk2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">neurosdk2</a> (MIT wrapper around BrainBit&#x27;s closed core library): the headband connection in the phone app.</li><li><a href=\"https://pypi.org/project/pyneurosdk2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pyneurosdk2</a> (MIT wrapper, same closed core): the headband connection on the laptop.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/holgern/pyedflib/blob/master/LICENSE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pyedflib</a> (BSD-3): writes the night&#x27;s BDF+ file.</li><li><a href=\"https://pub.dev/packages/flutter_foreground_task\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_foreground_task</a> (MIT): keeps the phone app recording with the screen off.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "sleep-scoring": {"title": "Sleep scoring (the night chart)", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>The morning after, two sleep models read the night&#x27;s file and label every 30 seconds as awake, light, deep or dream (REM) sleep. The result is a chart of the night (a hypnogram) plus sleep onset, time asleep and wake-ups.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Similar dry headbands matched lab scoring for sleep timing and for wake, dream and non-dream sleep, but this headband has never been checked against a lab recording, and the five-stage detail (light and deep sleep) is weak.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>AnySleep, the main model, accepts any set of electrodes (it learned from 19,909 nights). On lab recordings it scores about 0.68 with one channel and 0.73 with four (macro-F1: a 0-1 score averaged over the stages).</li><li>The closest dry headbands set the range. Dreem reached kappa 0.748 (kappa: agreement beyond chance, 0 to 1), while human experts agree with each other at 0.798. Muse-S reached 0.76. At home, dry ZMax nights averaged 0.45 and 10% of nights failed.</li><li>A fair target here is kappa 0.6-0.75 on nights with good contact, with light sleep (N1, the drowsy first stage) staying poor.</li><li>Big pretrained &quot;brain foundation models&quot; lost or tied on every test on headbands like this one; on two-channel sleep data, plain hand-made measures tied the big models.</li><li>Retraining a model on lab nights reshaped to look like the device added 0.058 kappa in an ear-EEG study, and one labelled night per person added 2-4.5 accuracy points.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>Nobody has scored sleep from a BrainBit next to a lab sleep study. Until that is done, the chart is a first guess.</li><li>Light sleep (N1) is missed by every headband. Dry electrodes may record extra slow waves, which can push light sleep into &quot;deep&quot;, so treat deep-sleep minutes with care.</li><li>When the two models agree, that shows they are consistent, not that they are right.</li><li>YASA was trained on a top-of-head channel; back and side electrodes are outside its training.</li><li>AnySleep looks about 7 minutes forward and back, so the night&#x27;s chart is scored afterwards. Read live, 2 minutes behind the newest data (the nap coach does this), it agreed with its own full-night scoring on most of one recorded night, a little less at the live edge.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>AnySleep (three runs averaged, all four channels, plus a filter that removes slow drift) is the main model; YASA on the O1-T3 pair is the second opinion on the laptop. Where they disagree, the chart shows low confidence.</li><li>The phone scores the night itself after you press Stop, with the same three AnySleep runs made smaller for the phone; on a test stretch of a recorded night they gave the same stage as on the laptop for every 30 seconds. The phone goes through the night about an hour at a time to save memory, and twice: first it learns how much to trust each electrode over the whole night, then it scores each hour that way. On the three recorded nights that gave the laptop&#x27;s stage for every thirty seconds (hour by hour alone had changed about 3 in 100). The phone has no second opinion.</li><li>A 30-second stretch missing more than 5 seconds of data is not scored: missing data never becomes &quot;awake&quot; or &quot;deep&quot;.</li><li>Noise is shown, not hidden: the report says how much of your sleep the model read through a noisy signal (movement, jaw muscle or a loose electrode) or with no usable electrode, and a Signal line under the chart marks where. Nobody has tested how well the model copes with that noise on this headband, so treat those stretches with care. On the first recorded night 13 % of the sleep was noisy and 3 % had no usable electrode.</li><li>An upgrade (AnySleep fine-tuned on public nights reshaped to this headband&#x27;s channels) is adopted only if it adds at least 0.03 kappa on nights it has not seen.</li><li>Big foundation models only in a later side-by-side test; none is used now.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.14461v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AnySleep preprint</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7751170/fullTextXML\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dreem dry headband vs experts (Arnal 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://espace2.etsmtl.ca/33349/1/Lina-JM-2026-33349.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Muse-S sleep study (Lanthier 2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.06.01.26354591v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ZMax home nights</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.29320v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BrainCodes: tiny model beat big ones on the 4-electrode Muse</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.24519v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Zare 2026: two-channel sleep, hand-made measures tie big models</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ece.au.dk/fileadmin/ece/Center/Ear-eeg/News_files/EMBC2025papers/LebieckaJohansen2025_BenefitsOfDifferentStrategiesToAdaptSleepScoringModelsFromScalpToEarEEG.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lebiecka-Johansen 2025: adapting sleep models to ear-EEG</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11679099/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mikhaylov 2024: dry electrodes record extra slow power</a></li><li><a href=\"https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12561290\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wang 2025: dry 8-site band, light sleep missed</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2506.08574v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SLEEPYLAND: model agreement vs accuracy</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/dslaborg/AnySleep\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AnySleep</a> (MIT; code and weights): the main sleep model.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ONNX Runtime</a> 1.28 (MIT) through <a href=\"https://github.com/masicai/flutter_onnxruntime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_onnxruntime</a> 1.9.0 (MIT): runs AnySleep on the phone.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/raphaelvallat/yasa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">YASA</a> 0.8.1 (BSD-3): the second opinion.</li><li><a href=\"https://pypi.org/project/mne/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MNE</a> (BSD-3): reads and filters the recording.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/holgern/pyedflib/blob/master/LICENSE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pyedflib</a> (BSD-3): reads and writes BDF+ files.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "smart-alarm": {"title": "Smart alarm", "grade": "weak", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Off by default. Before a night you set a &quot;wake me by&quot; time and a window that ends there (15, 20, 30 or 45 minutes). Inside the window the app wakes you at the first moment your sleep reads as light, dream (REM) or awake, and not just after deep sleep. If no such moment comes, it wakes you at the set time, like a plain alarm.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: weak</h3><p>The sleep model can read deep sleep from these electrodes (graded &quot;some&quot; in the nap coach and sleep scoring notes), but the claim that waking from light sleep leaves you less groggy than a plain alarm is weakly supported: studies of the sleep stage at waking disagree, and the tests of smart alarms found no gain.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Sleep inertia is the slow, foggy time after waking. A review found it rarely lasts more than 30 minutes unless you are short of sleep, and that waking from deep sleep (slow, large brain waves; stage N3) gives more of it than waking from light sleep.</li><li>A newer review is less sure: older studies linked waking from deep sleep to worse performance (one found a 41 % drop), several others found no link, and &quot;it is difficult to synthesize a clear conclusion&quot;. The worst grogginess is felt right away and most clears in 30 minutes, but full recovery takes at least an hour. It is worse after too little sleep and when you wake in your body&#x27;s night.</li><li>After normal 8-hour nights at the usual wake time, the stage people woke from made no measurable difference to how groggy they were or how fast it cleared. Most of them woke from dream or light sleep anyway, so the study says little about deep sleep; it does suggest that a plain morning alarm seldom lands in it.</li><li>A phone app that tracks movement in bed and promises to wake you from light sleep was tested in a sleep lab on 20 people. It named the stage correctly 45.9 % of the time. 14 of the 17 people asleep at the alarm were in light sleep, but light sleep filled about 60 % of their night, so that was no better than chance.</li><li>Four more phone apps were tested at home on 21 people. Both versions of Sleep Cycle were not reliable even at telling sleep from wake, and none of the four could find dream sleep.</li><li>A headband reads brain waves, which a phone on the mattress cannot. Dreem&#x27;s headband found 70 % of deep sleep live, and scoring with only the past costs 1-2 accuracy points against scoring afterwards.</li><li>Muse&#x27;s headband has a smart wake-up. Its maker reports that across about 6,200 nights, on the nights with enough sleep, waking from light sleep went with a better morning mood; and that how long people slept mattered far more. This is the maker&#x27;s own, unpublished data, with no comparison against a plain alarm.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>No study has shown that a smart alarm makes you less groggy than a plain alarm at the same time. In the one trial close to it (29 people, four lab nights), a bedroom system that started its wake-up light when a mattress sensor saw light sleep did no better than a beeping alarm on a reaction test, sleepiness or mood, and sleep on those nights was slightly more broken.</li><li>A smart alarm wakes you up to a full window early. That is sleep lost, and too little sleep makes grogginess worse. Nobody has measured whether the better moment pays for the lost minutes.</li><li>No study has tested an EEG headband alarm of this kind, and no one has checked this headband&#x27;s stages against a sleep lab.</li><li>The live stages are AnySleep, read 2 minutes behind the newest data. In the nap coach work they were compared with the model&#x27;s own full-night scoring on one recorded night; that shows the live version is consistent with the full one, not that either is right.</li><li>The drowsy first stage of light sleep (N1) is poorly scored everywhere, and the alarm acts on a single 30-second stretch, so some wake-ups will come at a moment that was not what the model said.</li><li>Replayed on one recorded night with 30-minute windows, it seldom woke anyone from what the full-night scoring called deep sleep, but it mostly rang in the window&#x27;s first minute: deep sleep is rare in the last hours of a night, so a smart alarm often just wakes you earlier. A shorter window keeps more of your sleep.</li><li>The 10-minute wait after deep sleep is this project&#x27;s own caution. No study sets that number.</li><li>Treat it as a pleasant way to wake up that may help a little, not as a proven fix for groggy mornings.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Before Start on the night screen, switch it on, set &quot;wake me by&quot; (for example 07:30) and the window (30 minutes first). The window ends at that time.</li><li>The window&#x27;s end is also set as a normal phone alarm at Start. It rings even if the phone closed the app in the night, the sleep model was too slow or failed, or the headband&#x27;s signal was lost.</li><li>The headband records all night as always. The live sleep model runs only from 60 minutes before the window until it wakes you, to save battery. It is the nap coach&#x27;s model: every 30 seconds AnySleep scores the last 60 minutes of signal, prepared with only the past, and keeps the stage of the 30-second stretch 2 minutes back.</li><li>Inside the window it wakes you at the first stretch read as light sleep, dream sleep or awake, but never within 10 minutes after a stretch read as deep sleep. If no such moment comes, it wakes you at the window&#x27;s end, whatever the stage.</li><li>The sound is the nap coach&#x27;s three soft rising notes, a little louder every 20 seconds. After 3 minutes without &quot;I&#x27;m awake&quot;, the phone&#x27;s own alarm sound takes over. &quot;I&#x27;m awake&quot; (on the lock screen, the notification or in the app) stops it. The recording goes on until you stop the night.</li><li>The morning report says when it woke you, why (light sleep, dream sleep, awake, or the window&#x27;s end), when you tapped &quot;I&#x27;m awake&quot;, and what the full-night scoring says about that minute.</li><li>If the sleep model causes any trouble (an error, too much time or memory), it switches itself off and the plain alarm stays. The recording is never affected.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1053/smrv.2000.0098\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tassi &amp; Muzet 2000: sleep inertia review</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.dovepress.com/sleep-inertia-current-insights-peer-reviewed-fulltext-article-NSS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hilditch &amp; McHill 2019: sleep inertia review</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2869.1999.00128.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jewett 1999: grogginess after normal nights, no effect of stage</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4481053\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bhat 2015: a light-sleep alarm app against a sleep lab</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/jsr.12935\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fino 2020: four phone sleep apps against home sleep recording</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10969141\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Campanella 2024: a smart bedroom alarm did no better</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5853451/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Debellemaniere 2018: Dreem&#x27;s live deep-sleep detection</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10017438/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GSSC: past-only scoring 1-2 % below</a></li><li><a href=\"https://choosemuse.com/blogs/news/smart-wakeup-how-muse-wakes-you-when-your-brain-is-ready\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Muse Smart Wakeup</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.14461v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AnySleep preprint</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/dslaborg/AnySleep\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AnySleep</a> (MIT): the sleep model, its three runs exported to ONNX for the phone.</li><li><a href=\"https://pub.dev/packages/flutter_onnxruntime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_onnxruntime</a> (MIT) with ONNX Runtime (MIT): runs the model on the phone.</li><li>Android&#x27;s own alarm clock (AlarmManager) and media player, part of Android: the alarm at the window&#x27;s end and the sounds.</li><li>This project&#x27;s own live preparation and wake rule (MIT); the Python reference is <code>analysis/live_stager.py</code> and <code>analysis/smart_alarm.py</code>.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "sound-for-states": {"title": "Sound for states (binaural beats, isochronic tones, pulsed noise)", "grade": "weak", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet heard on a phone) and on a laptop page. Pick a state (fall asleep, relax, focus, break, wake up) and a calm sound: rain, pink or brown noise, ocean. A beat is an optional extra: pulsed noise, isochronic tones or binaural beats. &quot;No beat&quot; plays the same sound at the same loudness, so you can compare. With the headband on, a quiet minute comes first, then the app shows what your brain does during the sound. &quot;Fall asleep&quot; stops by itself once you are probably asleep. Without the headband the sound plays by its timer. Over the weeks the app compares the sounds you used for each state, and a fair test can let chance pick between two sounds.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: weak</h3><p>The hearing part of the brain reliably follows a pulse, but no kind of sound has strong evidence that it moves your whole brain into a chosen state.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Binaural beats (a slightly different tone in each ear) exist only inside the brainstem and need stereo headphones. Isochronic tones (a tone switched on and off) and pulsed noise carry the pulse in the sound itself and work on a speaker.</li><li>The hearing area&#x27;s echo of a pulse is stronger for pulses in the sound than for binaural beats, yet in a 16-person study people felt no more relaxed with either.</li><li>Of 14 binaural-beat EEG studies, 5 support brain-wave &quot;entrainment&quot; (the brain falling into step), 8 contradict it and 1 is mixed; none showed it in the focus (beta) range.</li><li>Nature sounds lowered stress by a medium amount across 36 publications (g = -0.60; g is an effect size where about 0.2 is small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large), helping stressed people most and slightly bothering people already relaxed.</li><li>Pink noise (a soft, rain-like hiss) for falling asleep was positive in 9 of 11 small studies, but reviewers rate the evidence weak or very low.</li><li>White or pink noise helps people with ADHD a little (g = 0.25) and slightly hurts everyone else (about -0.21). Focus music pulsed 16 times a second rests on one company-run paper with a borderline result.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>&quot;Puts your brain into alpha / theta / delta&quot;, &quot;boosts memory&quot;, &quot;treats insomnia, anxiety or ADHD&quot;, &quot;clinically proven&quot;, &quot;40 Hz for brain health&quot;: none is supported, and the app never says them.</li><li>An audit of about 7,000 online binaural tracks found only 7.5% delivered what listeners expected.</li><li>No direct sound study exists for short breaks or for waking up.</li><li>The two trials claiming beats add something to music are industry-linked, and one had no music-only group.</li><li>The sound can fake its own result: the brain&#x27;s echo shows up at the pulse rate, and a tone leaking from earbuds can land in the alpha band of the recording (a 240 Hz tone shows as 10 Hz at 250 samples a second).</li><li>Finding which sound works for you takes about 3-6 weeks of daily sessions per state; a 2-minute difference in time to fall asleep is barely detectable in a month.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Calm sound first: each state starts with a bed and no beat (fall asleep: pink noise; relax: ocean; focus, break and wake up: rain). A beat is an extra you choose.</li><li>Beats per state: fall asleep slides from 10 to 3 Hz over 30 min, relax 10 Hz, focus 16 Hz, break 10 to 8 Hz over 5 min, wake up 10 to 18 Hz over 5 min. The slide is smooth (the same ratio each minute).</li><li>Every sound is set to the same loudness (-23 LUFS, the broadcast loudness measure), so &quot;beat or no beat&quot; is never &quot;louder or quieter&quot;. A beat plays 6 dB above the bed under it, so the bed does not fill its gaps.</li><li>Tone pitch 200 Hz (allowed: 150, 200, 300, 400 Hz): nothing between 220 and 280 Hz, which would fold into the brain-wave bands at 250 samples a second.</li><li>Isochronic pulses have soft edges (12 ms, or a fifth of the pulse when faster) and swing down to -20 dB; pulsed noise swings to -10.5 dB. Fades: 45 s in for sleep, 6 s for the others; out over 2.5 min for sleep, 20 s for the others, 3 s when you press Stop.</li><li>With the headband: a silent quiet minute first (60 clean seconds, 2 min at most). Then alpha (8-12 Hz) and slow waves (4-7 Hz) at the back of the head against that minute, every second the electrodes are clean. The beat&#x27;s rate and twice it (each ± 1 Hz) are left out, because the brain&#x27;s echo of the beat would count as &quot;more alpha&quot;.</li><li>The echo itself is shown as an echo: expected, not a sign that your brain &quot;tuned in&quot;, and possibly sound leaking into the headband from earbuds.</li><li>Fall asleep: the drift-off detector watches. Once you are probably asleep the sound fades out over 2.5 min; it is silent 45 min after lights off whatever happens. Its EEG is kept with the night, and the night recording can start by itself when the sound has stopped.</li><li>Without the headband (or while the night records) the sound simply plays for its minutes.</li><li>After each session: one question, &quot;did it help you get there?&quot; (1-5). Each session is saved with its sound, timings, readout and answer.</li><li>What works for you: one number per session for each state. Fall asleep: minutes from lights off until probably asleep (the detector, else that night&#x27;s sleep report; still awake at 45 minutes counts as 45). Relax: alpha against the quiet minute. Break: slow waves, that is drowsiness. Focus and wake up: your answer, which is weak because you knew what played. A sound shows its median and middle half from 14 sessions with a number: the research&#x27;s floor for any pattern seen together (14 with, 14 without), stricter than the 3 + 3 or 7 + 7 of commercial apps. It is seen together, not tested: you chose the sound, and those days can differ in other ways.</li><li>A fair test: for one state the app draws one of two sounds for each day, in shuffled blocks of 4 days with both sounds on every weekday, for 4, 6 or 8 weeks; the plan is frozen before day 1. At the end it uses the self-experiments&#x27; randomisation test, which counts only the draw the app itself made, so a difference cannot come from the days you happened to choose. It needs 5 days of each sound with a number. You still hear which sound plays, so expectation can be part of any difference. The research&#x27;s adaptive rule (play the better sound more often) found the best sound no faster than plain rotation, so the app keeps to fixed blocks.</li><li>Keep the volume low, never while driving, and prefer the phone&#x27;s speaker to earbuds overnight.</li><li>On the laptop page: the same presets with beats on by default; its markers go into the recording.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10198548/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Binaural beats review, 14 studies (Ingendoh 2023)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.eneuro.org/content/7/2/ENEURO.0232-19.2020\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Orozco Perez 2020: monaural vs binaural echo</a></li><li><a href=\"https://carleton.ca/biology/2021/prof-rachel-buxtons-research-finds-nature-sounds-can-alleviate-stress-improve-mood-and-enhance-cognitive-performance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nature sounds review summary (Buxton 2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5366899\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gould van Praag 2017: natural sounds and stress</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34964434\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pink noise for sleep (Capezuti 2022)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.em-consulte.com/article/1421191/article/noise-as-a-sleep-aid-a-systematic-review\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Noise as a sleep aid (Riedy 2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11283987/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Noise and attention (Nigg 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11499863/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Brain.fm pulsed music (Woods 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/ICWSM/article/view/35806\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ICWSM 2025 audit of online binaural tracks</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6986749\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Alarm sounds and grogginess (McFarlane 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8906590\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mallik and Russo 2022</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1539823/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Venkatesan 2025</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3214253\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Audio leaking into EEG (Campbell et al.)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14156\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Thompson sampling in practice (Meier 2023)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications-detail/safe-listening-devices-and-systems-a-who-itu-standard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WHO-ITU safe listening standard</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>No outside library in the page: the browser&#x27;s built-in Web Audio makes every sound.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/alnitak/flutter_soloud\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">flutter_soloud</a> (MIT): plays the phone app&#x27;s sound, which the app makes itself, sample by sample; nothing is downloaded.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/jaimebg/Ambio\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ambio</a> (CC0 sound recordings): a source of nature sounds that may be shipped.</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/lm7137/SBaGenX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SBaGenX</a> and the other open beat generators are GPL: ideas only, no code copied.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "study-timing": {"title": "Study timing (evening study, morning review)", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet run on a phone), and only when you switch it on in Settings: the assistant&#x27;s proposal, not a feature you asked for. A reminder in the evening, 1 to 2 hours before your usual bedtime (&quot;Good time to learn something you want to keep&quot;), and one in the morning, 30 to 60 minutes after you usually wake (&quot;Try to recall what you studied last night, before looking&quot;). One tap each way records &quot;studied tonight: yes or no&quot; and &quot;recalled: well, some or a little&quot;. It does not teach anything and does not use the headband.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>The headband plays no part. What backs the idea is one small study of evening learning followed by a morning review, plus general findings on sleep and memory; the effect for you, and for your kind of material, is unknown, and the app can only help you look at your own days.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>The main study (Mazza 2016, 40 people, 16 foreign-language word pairs): people who learned in the evening and relearned the next morning needed about half the practice to get every pair right again (3.05 against 5.80 tries), remembered more a week later (15.2 against 11.3 of 16) and still more at 6 months (8.7 against 3.4 of 16), than people who learned in the morning and relearned in the evening.</li><li>Those numbers are very large and come from small groups of 20, so the true effect is probably smaller. The two groups also learned at different times of day (the authors checked sleepiness and morning or evening type and found no difference), and nobody&#x27;s sleep was recorded.</li><li>Naps help remembering facts a little: across 54 studies the effect was small to medium (a standardised difference of about 0.38).</li><li>&quot;Sleep improves practised skills&quot; (such as finger-tapping sequences) largely disappears once the studies&#x27; design flaws are removed, so this note makes no claim about skills.</li><li>No study of sleep and programming skill was found, and none of a reminder like this one.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>The reminder is not the research. In the study people learned, slept and relearned to a set goal; a notification and a self-rating are the app&#x27;s own way to nudge the same order, and nothing shows the nudge changes what you do or what you remember.</li><li>&quot;Recalled: well, some or a little&quot; is how you feel it went, not a test. People misjudge their own memory in both directions, so use it to compare your own days, not to grade yourself.</li><li>Why the morning step is &quot;recall first, then look&quot; is the app&#x27;s design, not the study&#x27;s (which was relearning). The research this project read did not cover recall practice or spacing, so the note makes no claim about them.</li><li>The best gap between studying and bed was not researched. 1 to 2 hours before bed is a planning value, and what you study (the study used word lists) may behave differently.</li><li>Whether studying late costs you sleep was not researched here. The app does not know what or how long you study: keep to what you would do anyway.</li><li>Seen together, not tested: days you study in the evening may differ in other ways (a calm day, more energy). The self-experiment &quot;Study in the evening or the morning&quot; (Trends, Experiments) draws the days at random and compares your recall the next morning. On a morning-study day the recall comes 24 hours after studying, not 12, which can favour the evening; the experiment cannot remove that difference, so keep it in mind when you read its answer.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Off until you switch it on (Settings, Study reminders). The page says it is the assistant&#x27;s idea, and it can be switched off or deleted there.</li><li>Times: the evening reminder 90 minutes before your usual bedtime, rounded down to the quarter hour; the morning reminder 45 minutes after your usual wake-up, rounded up to the quarter hour. &quot;Usual&quot; is the middle of the last 14 days of recorded sleep, from 3 nights; until then 21:00 and 08:30. You can set either time by hand and go back to following your sleep.</li><li>Never in the quiet hours: automatic times are moved inside your waking hours (the Sharp hours window if you have one saved, else the one your sleep suggests), and a time you set must be inside them. Never during a night recording, a sound session or a session on screen: the reminder is skipped and logged. One that arrives over an hour late is dropped; one you miss goes after an hour; &quot;In 15 min&quot; puts it off once.</li><li>The reminders use the same alarm setup as Sharp hours: no exact-alarm permission, so they can be a few minutes late, and on a Xiaomi they need Autostart and no battery limits.</li><li>Say &quot;no&quot; to &quot;Did you study something tonight?&quot; and the next morning&#x27;s reminder is skipped (nothing to recall); &quot;Not yet&quot; leaves the question for later on the page.</li><li>The log: tonight&#x27;s yes or no and the morning&#x27;s well, some or a little, kept only in the app&#x27;s private folder and taken by Export data. Trends shows the day&#x27;s recall (1 a little, 2 some, 3 well; the mean of the day&#x27;s answers), and a self-experiment can use it as its number.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797616659930\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mazza 2016: sleep after evening learning, morning relearning, 6-month follow-up</a></li><li><a href=\"https://medicine.nus.edu.sg/newsletters/issue45/science-of-life/how-naps-can-be-beneficial-for-you\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Leong, Lo and Chee 2022: naps and memory, 54 studies</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000009\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pan and Rickard 2015: sleep and motor sequence learning</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>None beyond this project&#x27;s own schedule and log (MIT); Android&#x27;s alarm and notification services share the Sharp hours setup, no extra library.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "trends": {"title": "Trends (sleep pressure, \"your usual\", forecasts)", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>A page in the phone app with a few numbers per day (sleep, jaw muscle bursts in the night, morning sleepiness and reaction time, mood, tension, the alpha numbers, practice, coffee and alcohol), each shown next to yesterday&#x27;s and your usual, plus a sleep-pressure curve built from your sleep log. Forecasts stay hidden until they prove useful for you.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Sleep pressure from your sleep log is a decades-old, well-tested model, and your usual range is plain counting; an EEG sleep-pressure number and next-day forecasts are weak, and no study shows the EEG improves forecasts.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>The two-process sleep model (pressure builds while you are awake and drains in sleep, plus the body clock) is behind the US Army&#x27;s alertness model, and sleep-tracker data are accurate enough as its input.</li><li>Its numbers: pressure builds on a time scale of about 18-21 hours awake (people range 14-26) and drains on a scale of 2-4 hours of sleep; caffeine&#x27;s half-life is about 5 hours (1.5-9.5 between people).</li><li>In the best head-to-head test of daily mood forecasts (145 people), the best model beat &quot;same as last time&quot; by only 5-8%, not significantly, and several did worse.</li><li>Showing a forecast can change the day: people falsely told they had slept badly felt less alert and more tired by evening. A range written in words was read right by 94% of people; the same range as a graphic was misread by 36%.</li><li>Late coffee steals sleep: 45 minutes on average; 400 mg six hours before bed cut measured sleep by 1.1-1.2 hours while diaries noticed only 41 minutes.</li><li>Wearable &quot;readiness&quot; scores are a warning: 14 composite scores from 10 companies, none with a published formula.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>An EEG sleep-pressure meter: after a normal day the change at the back of the head is too small, and the closest wearable tests found no change and no link to how sleepy people felt.</li><li>EEG inside forecasts: no study found that sleep or morning EEG adds anything.</li><li>Famous &quot;tomorrow&#x27;s mood&quot; accuracies of 79-82% do not carry over: they threw out the in-between days and had no simple baseline to beat.</li><li>No EEG sign has been shown to warn one person ahead of illness, burnout or low mood.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>&quot;Your usual&quot;: the middle value of your last 28 days with a value, shown from 7 days. A day runs from 04:00 to 04:00; a night counts for the day you woke from it. No single brain score, no red or green health colours.</li><li>Arrows from 14 days, and only when a day is beyond your normal day-to-day spread (twice a robust spread) and beyond the smallest change that matters: 1 hour of sleep, 2 points on a rating, 10 % in reaction time, a quarter of your usual in jaw muscle bursts (a planning value). Each arrow says in words what it means, with your normal range.</li><li>Jaw muscle bursts are the sleep report&#x27;s count an hour asleep: a trend to compare your nights, not a test for teeth grinding (the jaw note says why).</li><li>&quot;Same as yesterday&quot;: every number shows yesterday&#x27;s beside it.</li><li>Sleep pressure comes from your sleep log (sleep periods of the phone&#x27;s sleep reports; builds with an 18-hour time scale while awake, drains with a 4-hour one asleep), labelled as an estimate. Your body clock and coffee are not in it, and it stops where a night is missing. An EEG-adjusted number appears only if it beats the log-only one on days it has not seen.</li><li>One forecast, tomorrow morning&#x27;s sleepiness (half yesterday, half your 28-day mean), stays hidden until 60 days of ratings exist and it beats &quot;same as yesterday&quot; by at least 5 % over the last 60 days; then it is shown in words, with a range and its track record, and only after that morning&#x27;s rating.</li><li>&quot;Unusual for you&quot; (its own note) says when a number has been outside your usual on 2 days in a row; it keeps the weeks after &quot;feeling ill&quot; out of its own usual.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37947051/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Alertness model with sleep trackers as input (Reifman 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27397562/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">US Army model with caffeine (Ramakrishnan 2016)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK223808/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Caffeine half-life (IOM 2001)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12827976\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fechtelpeter 2025: 7 models vs &quot;same as last time&quot;</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29989248/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gavriloff 2018: false &quot;you slept badly&quot; feedback</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/computer-science/articles/10.3389/fcomp.2020.590232/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Joslyn &amp; Savelli 2021: forecasts in words</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36870101/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Caffeine and sleep meta-analysis (Gardiner 2023)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3805807/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Caffeine 6 hours before bed (Drake 2013)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/teb-2025-0001/html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wearable readiness scores review (Doherty 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10901434/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Around-ear EEG after short sleep (van Klaren 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11744226\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Home EEG vs mood (Boon 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7266106\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SNAPSHOT mood forecasts (Taylor 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12347992\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Burnout EEG review (Chmiel &amp; Malinowska 2025)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/generated/statsmodels.tsa.statespace.structural.UnobservedComponents.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">statsmodels UnobservedComponents</a> (BSD-3): the main forecast model (a slowly drifting personal level plus a weekday effect).</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/amazon-science/chronos-forecasting\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Chronos-2</a> (Apache-2.0): a pretrained forecaster, only as an extra competitor.</li><li>The sleep-pressure model and the forecast are this project&#x27;s own code (MIT); the statsmodels and Chronos lines above are for later laptop comparisons, not in the app.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-18</p>"},
  "unusual-for-you": {"title": "Unusual for you", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet run on a phone; the assistant&#x27;s proposal, on unless you switch it off). It quietly compares your sleep length and your morning ratings with your own usual. When one of them has been well outside it on 2 days in a row, the Trends page says so in one plain sentence (&quot;Your sleep has been 2 h shorter than usual for 2 nights&quot;). It says your numbers moved, not why.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Simple personal rules like this caught most infections in smartwatch data; the same design on sleep length and ratings has not been tested by anyone, and no EEG number has been shown to warn of anything.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>The best-tested early-warning system is a simple rule. Stanford&#x27;s NightSignal compared each night&#x27;s resting heart rate with your running median and raised an alert after 2 nights in a row above it. It caught 80 % of 84 COVID cases, a median of 3 days before symptoms, and beat a change detector (72 %) and an hourly anomaly detector (69 %).</li><li>False alarms are the main cost: about 12 % of healthy days turned red, mostly after poor sleep, stress, alcohol, hard exercise, travel or a vaccine. Still, 73 % of people found the rate acceptable and could usually name the cause.</li><li>A per-day rule that is right 95 % of the time still flagged a third of people at least once in 6 days. Using the nights from 3 weeks to 1 week back as &quot;your usual&quot; keeps a slowly starting change from dragging it along.</li><li>Sleep behaviour carries the signal: colds and overreaching cut sleep by about 20-40 minutes, and before a relapse of depression sleep got less regular with more waking at night.</li><li>After an illness the body&#x27;s numbers can stay off for one to two weeks (longer after a hard COVID), so those weeks should not count toward &quot;your usual&quot;.</li><li>Fancier change detectors often do no better than &quot;never alarm&quot; on default settings; agreement between several simple signals helped more.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>EEG: no EEG number has been shown to warn one person of illness, stress, burnout or low mood. Inflammation raised the alpha frequency in one study while sleepiness lowers it, so a direction means nothing; a consumer EEG headband&#x27;s sleep numbers did not follow daily mood.</li><li>Sleep length and ratings: no study has tested this rule on them. The numbers below come from made-up people, not from you.</li><li>Small changes are missed: a cold&#x27;s 20-40 minutes are inside most people&#x27;s normal night-to-night swing.</li><li>Most cards will not be illness. A long average gap between false alarms still allows early ones.</li><li>Health alerts can feed worry in some people; that is why there is no notification and the switch is one tap away.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>The numbers: sleep length (from the headband&#x27;s sleep model), sleepiness, mood, tension and the reaction test, and, if you use Sharp hours, the day&#x27;s average sharpness and the day&#x27;s reaction time from it. Sleep length counts both ways; the others only the way that means strain (sleepier, lower mood, more tense, slower, less sharp). A Sharp-hours day counts once it is over. Steps are not recorded by the app.</li><li>Your usual for this card: the middle value of the 28 days with a value that end a week before (Whoop&#x27;s lagged window), needing 14 of them. &quot;Outside it&quot; is the Trends page&#x27;s arrow rule: at least twice your normal day-to-day spread and at least the smallest change that matters (1 hour of sleep, 2 rating points, 10 % in reaction time, 1 point in the day&#x27;s average sharpness).</li><li>A card: one number outside your usual on 2 days in a row (NightSignal&#x27;s rule); one missing day in between is bridged. It stays until a number comes back, then says &quot;Back in your usual range&quot; for 3 days.</li><li>Silent for the first 28 days with a number: it only notes what it would have said, and later tells you how often that was. A break of more than 14 days starts your usual again.</li><li>The headband&#x27;s own numbers (alpha speed, alpha strength, the eyes-closed rise, jaw muscle bursts) are judged both ways and written down, but never make a card: on made-up people they made 2.5 times the false cards (2.0 a month instead of 0.8) and no study shows they warn of anything.</li><li>Tested on 200 made-up people over a year each (planning values for their day-to-day wobble): 0.8 false cards a month (one every 6 weeks or so); 3 in 4 would see at least one false card in their first 8 weeks. Found: a cold-like week (sleep 40 minutes shorter, 2 points sleepier, mood 1 point lower) in 69 %, a week of low mood in 74 %, 4 nights of 2 hours less sleep in 57 %, of 1 hour less in 20 %, 5 days 1 point less sharp in 41 %, 5 days of 15 % slower reactions in the day in 55 %; usually 2 days after it began. A stricter rule (2 numbers together) almost never spoke, and missed nearly everything.</li><li>&quot;What might explain it?&quot; chips (late night, alcohol, travel, stress, hard exercise, vaccine, feeling ill, not sure) are for your own record. &quot;Feeling ill&quot; pauses the card for 2 weeks and keeps those days out of your usual; Resume now ends the pause.</li><li>Only on the Trends page and as one line on the main screen while there is a card; never a notification, never during a night. Every card says it is seen together, not tested, and not a diagnosis.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8799466/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NightSignal: real-time alerts from smartwatch heart rate (Alavi 2022)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7728254/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Whoop: breathing rate and a lagged baseline (Miller 2020)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://telehealth.org/news/smartwatches-detect-incomplete-recovery-days-after-patients-feel-better-study-finds/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Numbers stay off after an illness (Levi 2026, news report)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=DOI:10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2025.4453&amp;resultType=core&amp;format=json\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Depression relapse and sleep regularity (Tonon 2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06222\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Change detectors vs &quot;never alarm&quot; (van den Burg &amp; Williams)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18377\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Voting across detectors (Mozilla 2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.12660\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Long gaps between false alarms still allow early ones (2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2911704\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Inflammation raised the alpha frequency (van den Boogaard 2010)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.elsevier.es/en-revista-international-journal-clinical-health-psychology-355-articulo-exploring-bidirectional-within-subject-relationship-between-S169726002500105X\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EEG headband sleep vs daily mood (Boon, via a citing paper)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/digital-health/articles/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1694666\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Watch-based burnout prediction was weak (Dei Rossi 2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://news.unchealthcare.org/2024/07/wearable-devices-can-increase-health-anxiety-could-they-adversely-affect-health/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wearable alerts and health anxiety (Rosman 2024, news report)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/watch/apd15aa7ed96\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apple Vitals: two measures outside your range</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ouraring.com/blog/symptom-radar/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Oura Symptom Radar: shifted, not why</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/general-wellness-policy-low-risk-devices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">US FDA general wellness guidance (2026)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/StanfordBioinformatics/wearable-infection\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NightSignal, wearable-infection</a> (Apache-2.0): its alert rule (a running median, a fixed threshold, 2 nights in a row, one missing night filled in), re-implemented with attribution in this project&#x27;s own code (MIT): <code>analysis/unusual.py</code> and its phone port <code>app/lib/unusual/detector.dart</code>. No library is bundled.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "was-this-you": {"title": "Was this you?", "grade": "weak", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>In the phone app (not yet run on a phone; the assistant&#x27;s proposal, on unless you switch it off in Settings). After it has seen 10 of your check-ins (or 10 nights) on 5 different days, it asks &quot;Was it you?&quot; when a later one looks clearly unlike your usual brain signal. &quot;Someone else&quot; keeps that recording out of your trends, the unusual card and experiments; nothing is deleted or locked.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: weak</h3><p>A person&#x27;s eyes-closed alpha is stable over years, so a rough &quot;this looks like you&quot; is possible; but no study has tested a wearer check on this headband or any dry 4-electrode one, and this simple check was tested only on made-up people. It catches only a clearly different signal.</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>Eyes-closed alpha at the back of the head is one of the most repeatable things about a person&#x27;s EEG: its strength and its speed (the alpha peak frequency) repeat about as well over 5 years as over a week (ICC, the share of all variation that is between people, about 0.8). The slope of the background signal (the &quot;1/f slope&quot;) repeats less well (about 0.55-0.75).</li><li>The alpha speed jumps 1-2 Hz from second to second, so it has to be measured over minutes, never seconds.</li><li>Telling people apart from EEG works but is imperfect. In the largest test (345 people, about 6,000 sessions, wet 128-electrode lab caps) the error rate was 6.7 % a day later and 14 % a year later. With only the 4 electrodes of a Muse-like headband picked out of those caps it was 18 % on one short sample, and about 6 % with two enrolment days and four samples. That used a deep network trained on hundreds of people, not a simple rule.</li><li>A check that knows only its owner does much worse: in a 2021 study owner-only models were close to guessing (40-49 % error); models that had also seen other people did far better.</li><li>Alcohol slows the alpha speed (young binge drinkers by about 0.7 Hz), and sleeping pills and some antidepressants weaken alpha. So a borrowed headband and an unusual night of your own can look alike.</li><li>People do swap wearables: a fitness-tracker study was motivated by couples wearing each other&#x27;s devices.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>No wearer check has been tested on this headband or any dry 4-electrode headband. Dry contacts add noise the lab studies did not have, so the real numbers will be worse than theirs.</li><li>This check uses three simple numbers and only your own recordings (no network trained on other people), which is the weaker kind of check.</li><li>No recordings of other people were at hand to test it. On made-up people (their spread taken from the repeatability numbers above) it asked about a recording of the person themselves about once in 5 to 10 months of daily recordings, and noticed only 2 to 4 in 10 recordings of someone else.</li><li>On your own recordings so far it could not be tested: one check-in, and nights without Lights off or without a clear alpha peak at their start.</li><li>It cannot tell a moved headband, a loose electrode, alcohol, a new medicine or a short night from another person. That is why it only asks.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>The numbers: alpha speed, alpha strength and the background slope at the two back electrodes (O1 and O2), the morning check-in&#x27;s own measures. From a check-in&#x27;s 2 minutes with eyes closed; from a night&#x27;s awake minutes in the first 10 minutes after Lights off (a night without Lights off is not checked). Each needs a minute of clean signal and a clear alpha peak.</li><li>Check-ins and nights are learned apart. It is silent until 10 of a kind on 5 different days (&quot;Learning what your brain looks like: 3 of 10&quot;). Then each new one is compared with your newest 20 accepted ones.</li><li>The rule: how far each number is from your usual, in your own spreads (with a small floor so a very steady start is not too strict), combined; past 2.5 it asks. A recording joins your usual when it looked like you or when you said it was you, never when you said someone else.</li><li>&quot;Someone else&quot; is written into that night&#x27;s report or that check-in&#x27;s result and keeps it out of the trends, the unusual card and experiments. &quot;Someone else wore it&quot; is there on every recording too: when you know someone borrowed the headband, say so yourself. Undo takes an answer back.</li><li>Never a lock, a notification, a name or a health claim. Everything stays on the phone (Export data copies it out with everything else).</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.17866v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Large multi-session test of EEG as a biometric (Fallahi, Arias-Cabarcos and Strufe, arXiv 2501.17866 v3)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13456336/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Resting EEG over 5 years: alpha, alpha speed and the 1/f slope (Cerebral Cortex 2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.eneuro.org/lookup/doi/10.1523/ENEURO.0118-25.2025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The alpha speed jumps from second to second (eNeuro 2025)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10548-024-01067-x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Background slope reliability (Brain Topography 2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.usenix.org/system/files/sec21-arias-cabarcos.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Owner-only checks near guessing (USENIX Security 2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5995627\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Alcohol and the alpha speed in young binge drinkers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fncom.2018.00023/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Drugs and alpha at the back of the head (Lozano-Soldevilla 2018)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.cs.dartmouth.edu/dfk/research/cornelius-impedance/cornelius-impedance.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Couples swapping fitness trackers (Cornelius and Kotz)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.20454\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Two channels of home sleep EEG re-identify people (Fuhrmeister 2025)</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>No library or trained model: the rule is this project&#x27;s own code (MIT), <code>analysis/wearer_check.py</code> with its phone port <code>app/lib/wearer/check.dart</code>, on the morning check-in&#x27;s own measures (<code>scripts/morning_check.py</code>, <code>app/lib/morning/measures.dart</code>). The made-up back-test: <code>analysis/validate_wearer.py</code>.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"},
  "work-session": {"title": "Work session (drowsiness and break hints)", "grade": "some", "html": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>Built in the phone app (2026-11-19), not yet worn at work. You wear the headband while you work. After 4 minutes that become your awake baseline, every half minute the app reads the last 5 minutes as alert, getting drowsy or drowsy against it, and with a soft chime suggests a break after an hour worn, or sooner when drowsiness stays up. It notices breaks and shows whether drowsiness went down after one.</p><h3>Evidence for this headband: some</h3><p>Heavy eyelids (drowsiness) show at the back of the head and in the eyes, which these electrodes see, and on one recorded morning the level rose 8 minutes before the sleep model saw the owner doze; but that is one morning, and &quot;mental fatigue&quot; without sleepiness has no reliable sign at these electrodes (weak).</p><h3>What the research says</h3><ul><li>In a review of 21 studies of tiring tasks, brain waves grew with fatigue, theta most (g = 1.03) and alpha next (g = 0.85), the biggest changes over the top and front of the head, where this headband has no electrodes.</li><li>With eyes open, alpha at the back of the head rises as you get sleepy; with eyes closed it falls instead. Long nights awake also raise slow theta at the back of the head, but within a normal day the change is small.</li><li>A study close to this headband (gel electrodes around the ear, short nights and a work day) found no theta change, and the EEG did not follow how sleepy people felt or how fast they reacted.</li><li>Blinks get longer and more frequent over long computer work; heavy reading can hold blinking down instead.</li><li>Consumer headbands that try to detect drowsiness reach 31-79 % accuracy in a 2020 review of 46 studies, with accuracy defined in ways that cannot be compared.</li><li>Short breaks of up to 10 minutes make people feel a little less tired and more energetic (d = 0.35) but do not reliably improve demanding work; a fixed 25/5 timer did no better than breaks people chose themselves.</li></ul><h3>Where the data does not support it</h3><ul><li>On the owner&#x27;s recordings (the laptop&#x27;s <code>tool/work_check.dart</code>), theta at O1/O2 and the slow eye power at T3-T4 fell as he got drowsy, not rose: against the forehead reference both follow eye activity. So the app does not use them; it uses eyes-open alpha and how still the eyes are (fewer blinks and glances).</li><li>Still eyes can also mean deep focus. Still eyes alone never raise the level above alert; alpha must rise too.</li><li>Tested on one morning only: an hour with eyes open after a short, restless night, where the owner then fell asleep. The level read alert until 08:42, getting drowsy from 08:43 and drowsy from 08:48, and a drowsy hint would have come at 08:47, 9 minutes before the sleep model saw him doze; 2 hours awake in bed earlier gave no drowsy reading. Working days, coffee, talking and other people are untested.</li><li>With eyes closed (resting, or dozing at the desk) alpha falls as you drift off, so the level cannot follow it; the screen says &quot;Eyes closed&quot; instead, and 3 minutes of it in 6 bring a hint.</li><li>If your first 4 minutes were not your usual awake work (tests, talking, already drowsy), every later reading is off: with the morning&#x27;s baseline taken during eye tests the level only reached &quot;getting drowsy&quot;.</li><li>Taking the headband off and putting it back can shift the signal; the baseline stays the session&#x27;s first minutes.</li><li>&quot;Mental fatigue&quot; and &quot;focus&quot; are not measured: no study links these electrodes to either during real work.</li></ul><h3>How the app uses this</h3><ul><li>Every 30 seconds: alpha (8-12 Hz) at O1/O2 from clean seconds only (no muscle, talking, chewing, movement, blink or hole), and blinks plus sideways eye movements per 30 seconds; under 10 clean seconds the half minute is not read.</li><li>Your baseline: after 1 minute of settling in, the medians of the next 3 minutes. The index is the mean of alpha against it and eye stillness against it (both on a log2 scale); the level is the median of the last 5 minutes (needs 3 readable minutes): under 0.5 alert, from 0.5 getting drowsy, from 1.0 drowsy.</li><li>Closed eyes are told apart by alpha 3 times the baseline with no blink and at most one glance in 30 seconds; with the optional eyes-closed minute, the line is halfway (geometrically) between your open and closed alpha, at least 2 times (the owner&#x27;s closed eyes gave only 2.5 times his open-eye alpha). They are left out of the level.</li><li>Hints, the laptop&#x27;s break-hint rules: after 60 minutes worn, plus 15 minutes for each hint no break followed; or when the level read getting drowsy or more in 8 of the last 12 half minutes. At most 4 a day (04:00 to 04:00), 45 minutes apart, none 23:00-08:00; Snooze brings one back 20 minutes later. A soft chime and the words on the lock-screen card.</li><li>A break: On a break / Back, or the headband off (no usable signal) for 5 minutes. A break of 5 minutes or more resets the hour; after it the app compares the level before it with the first 5 minutes after it.</li><li>With each hint an optional one-tap &quot;How sharp do you feel?&quot; (the sharp-hours scale, 1 Foggy to 5 Very sharp), kept in the session&#x27;s file so a later check can ask whether the EEG agrees with you.</li><li>All planning values, set on one morning; your own weeks will tell.</li></ul><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://researchers.mq.edu.au/en/publications/the-influence-of-mental-fatigue-on-brain-activity-evidence-from-a/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tran 2020: EEG and mental fatigue, a meta-analysis of 21 studies</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2849788\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Rusterholz 2010: waking theta and sleep pressure</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10901434/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">van Klaren 2024: around-ear EEG after short nights</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11953059/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dry EEG in sleep-restricted trainees: theta and alpha rise</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-018-31577-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Blinks over prolonged computer work (Sci Rep 2018)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7593569\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">LaRocco 2020: drowsiness detection with consumer EEG, 46 studies</a></li><li><a href=\"https://doaj.org/article/c6f37b6ba159469296b2cb968b65505f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Albulescu 2022: micro-breaks, a meta-analysis</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12292963/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Smits 2025: Pomodoro against self-chosen breaks</a></li></ul><h3>Libraries and models</h3><ul><li>This project&#x27;s own detectors (<code>app/lib/detectors/</code>, MIT) and drowsiness rules (<code>app/lib/work/</code>, MIT); flutter_soloud (MIT) for the chime; Android&#x27;s foreground service for the locked phone.</li></ul><p>Checked: 2026-11-19</p>"}
};
